1
00:00:40,174 --> 00:00:42,584
[ Helicopter Whirring ]

2
00:01:11,805 --> 00:01:14,065
[ Door Opens, Closes ]

3
00:01:27,155 --> 00:01:30,755
Is everything
okay, sir?
Fine. Thank you.

4
00:01:30,858 --> 00:01:33,758
Here. Let me, uh--
That's not
necessary, sir.

5
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:39,370
[ Door Opens, Closes ]

6
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:08,702
[ Man Narrating ]
As I've gotten older, I realize
I'm certain of only two things.

7
00:02:08,796 --> 00:02:14,436
Days that begin with rowing
on a lake are better
than days that do not.

8
00:02:14,535 --> 00:02:19,305
Second,
a man's character
is his fate.

9
00:02:19,407 --> 00:02:23,207
And, as a student of history,
I find this hard to refute.

10
00:02:23,311 --> 00:02:27,381
For most of us,
our stories can be written
long before we die.

11
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:32,149
There are exceptions
among the great men of history,

12
00:02:32,253 --> 00:02:35,423
but they are rare,
and I am not one of them.

13
00:02:37,225 --> 00:02:39,125
[ Chuckles ]

14
00:02:39,227 --> 00:02:42,727
I am a teacher--
simply that.

15
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:45,270
I taught for 34 years.

16
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:48,066
One day I stopped teaching.

17
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:51,338
Those were the facts
of my life's chronicle.

18
00:02:51,439 --> 00:02:55,339
The last chapter
had been written.
My book was closed.

19
00:02:55,443 --> 00:02:57,653
[ Ringing ]

20
00:02:59,179 --> 00:03:01,179
Hello?
[ Ringing
Continues ]

21
00:03:03,917 --> 00:03:07,457
Hello. Hello?

22
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:15,829
[ Man On Intercom ]
Mr. Hundert?
Yes. Hello.

23
00:03:15,929 --> 00:03:19,099
Can you hear me, sir?
I can.

24
00:03:19,199 --> 00:03:22,099
Hello.
Mr. Bell wanted to make sure
you were comfortable.

25
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:24,442
I'm quite comfortable.
Thank you. And-And, please,

26
00:03:24,538 --> 00:03:27,738
thank Mr. Bell
for the beautiful
fruit basket.

27
00:03:27,841 --> 00:03:31,411
It's quite...
comprehensive.
Yes, sir.

28
00:03:31,512 --> 00:03:33,712
Mr. Bell also
wanted me to tell you
he's added an event...

29
00:03:33,814 --> 00:03:36,224
on the morning
following the competition--

30
00:03:36,317 --> 00:03:38,287
a breakfast
in your honor.

31
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:42,861
Mr. Hundert?
Yes--
Yes.

32
00:03:42,956 --> 00:03:44,856
Th-Thank you.
Thank you.

33
00:03:44,958 --> 00:03:48,598
The others will be
heading down to the bar
in 20 minutes, sir.

34
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:50,896
Uh, very well.
Twenty minutes.

35
00:03:50,998 --> 00:03:54,538
In the bar.
I'll be there.

36
00:03:54,635 --> 00:03:56,465
Th--

37
00:04:07,147 --> 00:04:11,487
[ Hundert's Voice ]
I couldn't tell you exactly
why I had agreed to come.

38
00:04:11,585 --> 00:04:15,455
I'm not sure I knew.
It had something to do
with hope.

39
00:04:18,091 --> 00:04:20,591
The young men that were
gathering here were among

40
00:04:20,694 --> 00:04:24,474
the most successful
and influential
of their generation.

41
00:04:24,565 --> 00:04:26,725
But I knew them
before any of that.

42
00:04:26,834 --> 00:04:29,344
I knew them as my students...

43
00:04:29,437 --> 00:04:32,407
at Saint Benedict's
School for Boys.

44
00:04:32,506 --> 00:04:36,606
[ Bell Tolling ]

45
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:42,780
[ Tolling Continues ]

46
00:05:18,519 --> 00:05:21,689
[ Applause ]

47
00:05:21,789 --> 00:05:24,289
[ Man ] Looking closer,
under the bishop's miter,

48
00:05:24,392 --> 00:05:26,692
are the words, "Non sibi"...

49
00:05:26,794 --> 00:05:29,964
or "Not for oneself."

50
00:05:30,063 --> 00:05:33,303
This embodies our philosophy
at Saint Benedict's.

51
00:05:33,401 --> 00:05:37,711
The wisdom gained here
should be used for others...

52
00:05:37,805 --> 00:05:39,835
as well as for oneself.

53
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:44,650
And finally,
"Finis origine pendet."

54
00:05:44,745 --> 00:05:50,615
These three words
are emblazoned
across your hearts...

55
00:05:50,718 --> 00:05:53,088
under the great seal
of Saint Benedict's.

56
00:05:53,186 --> 00:05:55,456
It is our academy's motto.
Simply:

57
00:05:55,556 --> 00:05:57,756
"The end depends upon
the beginning."

58
00:05:57,858 --> 00:05:59,958
The end...

59
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:03,530
depends upon
the beginning.

60
00:06:03,631 --> 00:06:06,731
[ Boys Chattering, Laughing ]

61
00:06:17,144 --> 00:06:19,654
Hey, Greg.
How are you doin'?

62
00:06:27,821 --> 00:06:29,721
Hi, Mr. Hundert.
Mr. Ericks.

63
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:35,060
Ex-Excuse me.
Huh? What me?

64
00:06:35,162 --> 00:06:37,632
Yes, sir.
What is your name?
Uh, Louis.

65
00:06:37,731 --> 00:06:41,171
- Just Louis?
- Louis Masoudi, sir.

66
00:06:41,268 --> 00:06:44,868
Mr. Masoudi,
could you define the word
"path" for me?

67
00:06:44,972 --> 00:06:48,142
Well, there are several
definitions, I suppose.

68
00:06:48,241 --> 00:06:51,481
Would "a route along which
someone or something moves"
be among them?

69
00:06:51,579 --> 00:06:53,749
Yeah.

70
00:06:53,847 --> 00:06:56,377
Oh, yeah. No. Yeah.
I'm s-sorry, sir.

71
00:06:56,484 --> 00:07:00,994
Follow the path, Mr. Masoudi.
Walk where the great men
before you have walked.

72
00:07:01,088 --> 00:07:03,988
Yes, sir. It's, uh--
It's better for the grass.

73
00:07:04,091 --> 00:07:06,791
It's better for you.

74
00:07:06,894 --> 00:07:09,004
Mr. Hundert.
Mr. Ellerby.

75
00:07:09,096 --> 00:07:11,626
I wanted to thank you.
For what?

76
00:07:11,732 --> 00:07:15,342
Mr. Woodbridge let slip
that you were instrumental
in my procuring this job.

77
00:07:15,435 --> 00:07:18,235
Bene exeat, sir.
Bene meritus.

78
00:07:18,338 --> 00:07:20,408
It's always good to have
another friend of the classics.

79
00:07:20,508 --> 00:07:22,408
I'll tell you,
the way he talks about you--

80
00:07:22,510 --> 00:07:26,250
"Just listen to Mr. Hundert.
He'll be running the place
one day."

81
00:07:26,346 --> 00:07:29,546
- Your name, sir?
- Robert Brewster, sir.

82
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:32,450
- Very good. And you?
- Deepak Mehta, sir.

83
00:07:32,553 --> 00:07:34,963
And your name?
Eugene Field, sir.

84
00:07:35,055 --> 00:07:38,155
Field. And you?
Martin Blythe, sir?

85
00:07:38,258 --> 00:07:42,358
- Is that a question?
- Uh, no, sir.

86
00:07:42,462 --> 00:07:44,532
Uh, my name
is Martin Blythe, sir.

87
00:07:44,632 --> 00:07:47,002
Very good.
You all know
your own names.

88
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:50,270
Question is, do I?

89
00:07:50,370 --> 00:07:54,580
- Mister... Blythe.
- Me, sir?

90
00:07:54,675 --> 00:07:58,505
Yes, you.
Not Mr. Field to your right...

91
00:07:58,612 --> 00:08:02,852
or Mr. Grey to your left
or Mr. Diebel behind you.

92
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:06,250
You, sir.
Will you do me a favor and walk
to the back of this classroom...

93
00:08:06,353 --> 00:08:10,063
and read for us
the plaque which you see
hanging over that door?

94
00:08:17,765 --> 00:08:20,565
[ Clears Throat ]

95
00:08:20,668 --> 00:08:24,338
"I am Sh-Shu--"
[ Chuckles ]

96
00:08:24,437 --> 00:08:29,237
- "I am Sh-Shutruk-Nahhunte"?
- Very good.
A little louder, please.

97
00:08:29,342 --> 00:08:34,822
Yes, sir.
"'I am Shutruk-Nahhunte,
King of Ansham and Susa,

98
00:08:34,915 --> 00:08:37,075
"'sovereign
of the land of Elam.

99
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:41,894
I destroyed Sippar
and took the 'stel-ah' of--'"
"Stele."

100
00:08:41,989 --> 00:08:45,759
"'Took the stele of Naram-Sin
and brought it back to Elam,

101
00:08:45,859 --> 00:08:48,159
"where I erected it as
an offering to my god.'

102
00:08:48,261 --> 00:08:52,801
Shutruk-Nahhunte,
1158 B.C."

103
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:55,770
Well done, Mr. Blythe.
Thank you. You may be seated.

104
00:08:55,869 --> 00:08:57,909
"Shutruk-Nahhunte."
Is anyone familiar
with this fellow?

105
00:08:58,005 --> 00:08:59,965
Texts are permissible.

106
00:09:00,073 --> 00:09:02,913
But you won't find it there.

107
00:09:03,010 --> 00:09:06,450
Shutruk-Nahhunte! King!

108
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:11,176
Sovereign of the land of Elam!
Destroyer of Sippar!

109
00:09:11,284 --> 00:09:15,924
Behold, his accomplishments
cannot be found in any
history book. Why?

110
00:09:16,023 --> 00:09:19,393
Because great ambition
and conquest...

111
00:09:19,492 --> 00:09:21,432
without contribution...

112
00:09:21,528 --> 00:09:23,558
is without significance.

113
00:09:25,498 --> 00:09:27,938
What will
your contribution be?

114
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:32,339
How will history
remember you?

115
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:38,478
Shutruk-Nahhunte--
utterly forgotten.

116
00:09:38,578 --> 00:09:42,918
Unlike the great men
you see around you--
Aristotle,

117
00:09:43,016 --> 00:09:45,146
Caesar,

118
00:09:45,252 --> 00:09:47,192
Augustus,

119
00:09:47,287 --> 00:09:51,587
Plato, Cicero, Socrates--

120
00:09:51,692 --> 00:09:55,962
giants of history,
men of profound character.

121
00:09:58,531 --> 00:10:02,401
Men whose accomplishments
surpassed their own lifetime...

122
00:10:02,502 --> 00:10:06,042
and survive
even into our own.

123
00:10:06,139 --> 00:10:08,309
Their story...

124
00:10:08,408 --> 00:10:10,708
is our story.

125
00:10:13,947 --> 00:10:16,577
My name is Mr. Hundert.

126
00:10:16,684 --> 00:10:19,294
Welcome
to Western Civilization:

127
00:10:19,386 --> 00:10:22,316
The Greeks and the Romans.

128
00:10:22,422 --> 00:10:25,192
* [ Rock Guitar ]

129
00:10:25,292 --> 00:10:27,432
[ Boisterous Laughter ]

130
00:10:27,527 --> 00:10:29,457
Welcome
to Saint Benedict's!
[ Grunts ]

131
00:10:29,562 --> 00:10:32,202
* [ Continues ]
[ Boys Laughing ]

132
00:10:32,299 --> 00:10:35,339
Lights out
in five minutes,
gentlemen.

133
00:10:37,237 --> 00:10:40,407
It's awfully loud,
Mr. Jones.
Please turn it down.

134
00:10:40,507 --> 00:10:43,277
Did you--
Her titties are so--
After that, though--

135
00:10:43,376 --> 00:10:45,276
No. After that,
I was walking back
to get my books.

136
00:10:45,378 --> 00:10:47,548
And I was walking
along the grass, and Hundert
comes up to me and says,

137
00:10:47,647 --> 00:10:50,817
- "Mr. Masoudi" and I turned
around but I took care of it.
- Yeah, I know. Yeah.

138
00:10:50,918 --> 00:10:53,188
And then, he's like,
"Follow the path, Mr. Masoudi.

139
00:10:53,286 --> 00:10:55,656
Walk where the great men
before you have walked."

140
00:10:55,756 --> 00:11:00,286
- Very good, Mr. Masoudi.
- I was just lettin' him know.
That's why I was tellin' him.

141
00:11:00,393 --> 00:11:02,403
To bed.

142
00:11:02,495 --> 00:11:04,925
Yeah. Right.
See you tomorrow.

143
00:11:05,032 --> 00:11:08,172
Lights out
in five minutes,
gentlemen.

144
00:11:08,268 --> 00:11:11,668
Just why am I--
Huh? No. I'm going.

145
00:11:14,241 --> 00:11:16,711
Good night, Mr. Blythe.
Yeah. Good night.
Good night.

146
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:18,710
Jesus Christ.
I am such an asshole.

147
00:11:24,284 --> 00:11:28,094
Carthaginians.
Uh--

148
00:11:28,188 --> 00:11:30,158
Yes, sir.

149
00:11:30,257 --> 00:11:33,027
Hamilcar Barca seems like
an outstanding commander.

150
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:36,556
Yes, who had the misfortune
of being on the losing side.

151
00:11:36,663 --> 00:11:39,373
You do realize that
Hamilcar Barca is not part
of the course reading list?

152
00:11:39,466 --> 00:11:40,926
Yes, sir.

153
00:11:42,202 --> 00:11:44,002
Very good.

154
00:11:45,372 --> 00:11:47,442
Lights out.

155
00:11:50,778 --> 00:11:52,708
* [ Continues ]

156
00:11:52,813 --> 00:11:55,823
9:00, gentlemen.
* [ Stops ]

157
00:11:55,916 --> 00:11:57,946
Good night.

158
00:12:04,858 --> 00:12:06,728
Hi.

159
00:12:14,334 --> 00:12:17,474
- Hello.
- Oh, hi.

160
00:12:18,872 --> 00:12:21,882
- [ Woman Laughs ]
- Welcome back.

161
00:12:21,975 --> 00:12:24,405
It's great to see you!
You too.
How are you?

162
00:12:24,511 --> 00:12:27,081
I'm fine.
You sure you're all right?

163
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,220
Ye-Yeah.

164
00:12:29,316 --> 00:12:32,886
What did you think
of Athens?
Oh. I loved it.

165
00:12:32,986 --> 00:12:35,956
I knew you would.
Tony hated it,
but you know the British.

166
00:12:36,056 --> 00:12:38,356
"Too many Greeks."
[ Laughs ]

167
00:12:38,458 --> 00:12:42,328
Tony's Tony.
Here.

168
00:12:42,429 --> 00:12:44,729
I told the man in the store
I wanted something...

169
00:12:44,832 --> 00:12:47,842
for a very
serious scholar
of antiquity.

170
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:55,980
[ Both Laughing ]

171
00:12:57,377 --> 00:12:59,447
The Parthenon.

172
00:12:59,546 --> 00:13:02,616
- It's smaller
than I remember it.
- [ Laughs ]

173
00:13:02,715 --> 00:13:05,115
I don't have one of these.
Thank you.

174
00:13:05,218 --> 00:13:07,248
I found it in the agora.

175
00:13:07,354 --> 00:13:10,924
Tony couldn't understand
why I wanted to go there.
It's a little touristy now,

176
00:13:11,024 --> 00:13:14,064
but I kept hearing
your voice in my head.

177
00:13:14,161 --> 00:13:16,601
And I kept seeing it
exactly the way
you described it,

178
00:13:16,696 --> 00:13:19,766
with Socrates teaching
right in front of me.

179
00:13:19,867 --> 00:13:23,497
And... there was Tony,
falling asleep
from the heat and ouzo,

180
00:13:23,603 --> 00:13:25,773
and me eating
my shish kabob.

181
00:13:25,873 --> 00:13:28,943
I thought about
you a lot. Um--

182
00:13:29,042 --> 00:13:31,642
- I wished you were there.
- It would've been
a little odd though...

183
00:13:31,744 --> 00:13:33,784
with you and me
and Socrates...

184
00:13:33,881 --> 00:13:35,821
and your sleeping husband.

185
00:13:37,550 --> 00:13:39,820
Yeah. A little.

186
00:13:55,869 --> 00:13:58,009
[ Hundert ]
And with the monarchy's demise,
two new systems...

187
00:13:58,105 --> 00:14:00,835
of government--
the first, ruled by the few,
known as?

188
00:14:00,941 --> 00:14:04,011
Mr. Brewster.
Uh, tyranny?

189
00:14:04,111 --> 00:14:07,011
In spirit, perhaps,
but etymologically, no.

190
00:14:07,114 --> 00:14:09,024
More precisely, oligarchy.

191
00:14:09,116 --> 00:14:12,386
Tyranny is what we have
in this classroom. It works.
[ Laughing ]

192
00:14:14,354 --> 00:14:17,024
Mr. Blythe?
Antisthenes: "We must
have richness of soul."

193
00:14:17,124 --> 00:14:21,494
Oh, it was, uh, Heraclitus,
when he said a man's character
is his fate or-or his destiny.

194
00:14:21,594 --> 00:14:24,564
Precisely.
"Not to know what happened
before you were born...

195
00:14:24,664 --> 00:14:26,874
is to be forever a child."

196
00:14:26,967 --> 00:14:29,237
[ Students Chattering ]

197
00:14:32,072 --> 00:14:35,582
[ Woodbridge ]
Mr. Julius Caesar...

198
00:14:35,675 --> 00:14:38,145
is a Saint Benedict's tradition.

199
00:14:38,245 --> 00:14:41,705
It is a contest
held each year
in two phases.

200
00:14:41,814 --> 00:14:43,884
The first,
is a series of quizzes...

201
00:14:43,984 --> 00:14:46,424
that will narrow the field
of competitors to three.

202
00:14:46,519 --> 00:14:48,659
The second
is a public tournament...

203
00:14:48,755 --> 00:14:50,985
where these three
will take the stage
at Saint Benedict's Hall...

204
00:14:51,091 --> 00:14:53,531
and answer questions
about Roman history.

205
00:14:53,626 --> 00:14:55,896
The last man standing
will be declared the winner...

206
00:14:55,996 --> 00:14:59,266
and crowned
Mr. Julius Caesar.

207
00:14:59,366 --> 00:15:02,436
- Your father was a winner
was he not, Mr. Blythe?
- Yes, sir.

208
00:15:02,535 --> 00:15:06,235
Mm-hm.
Would you point him out
to us, please?

209
00:15:06,339 --> 00:15:08,509
[ Woodbridge ]
Ah.

210
00:15:08,608 --> 00:15:11,578
A noble honor indeed.

211
00:15:11,678 --> 00:15:15,378
Well, carry on,
gentlemen.

212
00:15:15,482 --> 00:15:17,322
Thank you,
Mr. Woodbridge.

213
00:15:22,389 --> 00:15:24,459
[ Bell Tolling ]

214
00:15:28,328 --> 00:15:32,868
Finally, Caesar arrives
at the Rubicon,

215
00:15:32,966 --> 00:15:35,236
a small little river
which divides-- Mr. Field?

216
00:15:35,335 --> 00:15:38,035
Cisalpine Gaul
from Italy.
Correct.

217
00:15:38,138 --> 00:15:42,178
He gathers his one legion
around him. A legion comprised
of how many men-- Mr. Grey?

218
00:15:42,275 --> 00:15:46,405
6,000?
6,000 men.
Pompey has 10 legions...

219
00:15:46,513 --> 00:15:50,083
with the support
of the Senate, and Caesar
is undaunted by these odds.

220
00:15:50,183 --> 00:15:53,793
Suddenly-- impulsively--

221
00:15:53,886 --> 00:15:56,656
Caesar cries out! Mr. Mehta.

222
00:15:56,756 --> 00:16:00,526
- "Alea jacta est."
- "The die is cast!"

223
00:16:00,627 --> 00:16:04,127
He crosses the Rubicon
only to find his enemies
have fled.

224
00:16:04,231 --> 00:16:06,401
He chases Pompey
all around
the Mediterranean.

225
00:16:06,499 --> 00:16:10,239
Finally,
he arrives in Alexandria,
where the king of Egypt--

226
00:16:10,337 --> 00:16:13,307
- Mr. Masoudi?
- Ptolemy XIII?

227
00:16:13,406 --> 00:16:17,176
Ptolemy XIII...
presents him with a gift--

228
00:16:17,277 --> 00:16:20,647
the head of Pompey.
[ Knocking ]

229
00:16:24,251 --> 00:16:27,121
Mr. Hundert,

230
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:29,460
I'd like you to make
the acquaintance...

231
00:16:29,556 --> 00:16:32,386
of Mr. Sedgewick Bell.

232
00:16:32,492 --> 00:16:34,792
Pleasure
to meet you, Mr. Bell.

233
00:16:34,894 --> 00:16:37,864
Well, then,
carry on, Mr. Hundert.

