Seriously, though,
can you in fact...

464
00:30:33,032 --> 00:30:36,642
- name any of the emperors?
- I know four.

465
00:30:36,735 --> 00:30:42,265
- Very well.
- Uh, John, Paul,
Ringo and, uh,

466
00:30:42,374 --> 00:30:45,814
- George.
- Mr. Bell.

467
00:30:45,912 --> 00:30:49,682
A word of warning.
As the great wit
Aristophanes once wrote--

468
00:30:49,781 --> 00:30:53,951
roughly translated--
"Youth ages, immaturity
is outgrown,

469
00:30:54,053 --> 00:30:58,463
"ignorance can be educated
and drunkenness sobered,

470
00:30:58,557 --> 00:31:01,587
"but stupid...

471
00:31:01,693 --> 00:31:04,133
lasts forever."

472
00:31:12,738 --> 00:31:16,338
Class, if you please,
chronologically--

473
00:31:16,442 --> 00:31:21,052
[ All ]
Tiberius, Caligula,
Claudius, Nero,

474
00:31:21,147 --> 00:31:23,547
Galba, Otho,

475
00:31:23,649 --> 00:31:26,249
Vitellius, Vespasian,

476
00:31:26,352 --> 00:31:28,392
Titus, Domitian,

477
00:31:28,487 --> 00:31:32,117
Nerva, Trajan, Hadrian,

478
00:31:32,224 --> 00:31:36,704
Antonius Pius,
Marcus Aurelius,

479
00:31:36,795 --> 00:31:39,595
Commodus, Pertinax--
Thank you.
That will do.

480
00:31:41,733 --> 00:31:43,903
Please take your seat,
Mr. Bell.

481
00:31:45,837 --> 00:31:47,637
[ Chalk Clatters ]

482
00:32:06,192 --> 00:32:09,462
[ Knocking ]

483
00:32:10,729 --> 00:32:14,469
Come in.
[ Door Rattling ]

484
00:32:20,139 --> 00:32:23,939
Mr. Bell.
Mr. Hundert.

485
00:32:24,043 --> 00:32:26,183
Please, have a seat.

486
00:32:34,386 --> 00:32:37,516
Only child?

487
00:32:37,623 --> 00:32:39,393
Please be seated, Mr. Bell.

488
00:32:48,234 --> 00:32:50,444
Do you know why you're here?

489
00:32:50,536 --> 00:32:53,506
"Student of the day."

490
00:32:56,675 --> 00:33:00,375
I gave you "1"
for spelling your name
correctly.

491
00:33:00,479 --> 00:33:02,879
Mr. Bell,

492
00:33:02,981 --> 00:33:05,081
I don't know
what you think you're doing
at Saint Benedict's,

493
00:33:05,184 --> 00:33:07,154
but this
is unacceptable work.

494
00:33:07,253 --> 00:33:09,723
- You must apply yourself--
- You're not married,
are you, sir?

495
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:11,761
No. I am not.

496
00:33:11,857 --> 00:33:15,127
That's why you like
putting us all in togas,
right?

497
00:33:17,129 --> 00:33:20,269
I've made arrangements
to meet with your father.

498
00:33:23,469 --> 00:33:26,539
Is there anything
you'd like me to say to him?

499
00:33:32,678 --> 00:33:34,678
Tell him I said hello.

500
00:33:45,757 --> 00:33:47,627
[ Door Closes ]

501
00:34:07,446 --> 00:34:09,616
Well, how are you?
Good to see you.

502
00:34:09,715 --> 00:34:12,215
And you, sir.
Give me just a second.

503
00:34:13,919 --> 00:34:16,919
Who's that? Mm-hm.
Professor, come on in.

504
00:34:17,022 --> 00:34:20,292
Kathleen,
hold all my calls.
Thank you, Michael.

505
00:34:20,392 --> 00:34:22,532
Sit down, please.

506
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:30,570
Cubans.

507
00:34:30,669 --> 00:34:34,009
Don't tell the A.T.F.
[ Chuckles ]

508
00:34:34,106 --> 00:34:36,736
No. Not for me.
Thanks.
You're sure?

509
00:34:36,842 --> 00:34:39,482
Oh, yes, sir.
Don't know
what you're missin'.

510
00:34:45,684 --> 00:34:49,394
Senator--
Oh, here.
You'll enjoy this.

511
00:34:49,488 --> 00:34:52,018
This was given to me
by one of my constituents
this morning.

512
00:34:52,124 --> 00:34:54,034
Take a look at that.
[ Woman ]
Senator Bell's office.

513
00:34:54,126 --> 00:34:57,156
Hold it! Don't be
too fast there, partner.

514
00:34:57,263 --> 00:34:59,503
[ Chuckles ]
Quarter roll.
Ah.

515
00:34:59,598 --> 00:35:01,698
Here. Go on.
That was, uh--

516
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:05,770
Supposedly belonged to
the coachman of Robert E. Lee.
Really?

517
00:35:05,871 --> 00:35:08,111
Well, so he said.
[ Chuckles ]

518
00:35:08,207 --> 00:35:10,907
You're a history buff,
aren't ya?

519
00:35:11,009 --> 00:35:12,939
In a manner
of speaking, sir.
Yes.

520
00:35:13,044 --> 00:35:15,414
Well, take that.
That's yours.

521
00:35:15,514 --> 00:35:18,754
Oh, no. I couldn't.
Oh, take the damn thing!

522
00:35:18,850 --> 00:35:22,550
It's a gift.
For all you've done
for my son, Sedgewick.

523
00:35:22,654 --> 00:35:25,524
Thank you.

524
00:35:25,624 --> 00:35:28,134
[ Clicking ]

525
00:35:28,227 --> 00:35:30,257
So, to what
do I owe this honor?

526
00:35:30,362 --> 00:35:32,262
[ Clicking Continues ]
It's your son, Senator.

527
00:35:32,364 --> 00:35:35,674
Oh, Christ.
What the devil's
he done now?

528
00:35:35,767 --> 00:35:39,507
Sedgewick is clearly
an intelligent boy, but, sir,
he's not applying himself.

529
00:35:39,605 --> 00:35:41,705
He's not...
learning the material.
[ Drawer Sliding ]

530
00:35:41,807 --> 00:35:44,077
I need a match!
[ Woman ]
Yes, Senator. Right away.

531
00:35:44,176 --> 00:35:46,576
What material is that?
It's the Greeks
and the Romans--

532
00:35:46,678 --> 00:35:48,948
We've left the Republic
and entered the Empire--

533
00:35:49,047 --> 00:35:50,777
Here you are, sir.
Thank you.

534
00:35:50,882 --> 00:35:53,352
Shut the door
on your way out,
will you?

535
00:35:53,452 --> 00:35:55,322
We're up to
the reign of Augustus.

536
00:35:55,421 --> 00:35:57,661
You ought to be careful
with that thing, by the way.
You know, it still fires.

537
00:35:57,756 --> 00:36:00,526
Oh.
Sedgewick, it seems,

538
00:36:00,626 --> 00:36:03,026
is not paying attention
in class, sir.

539
00:36:03,128 --> 00:36:05,928
Nor is he doing
the assigned reading.

540
00:36:06,031 --> 00:36:08,201
Let me ask you something.

541
00:36:08,300 --> 00:36:10,870
What's the good
of what you're teachin'
those boys?

542
00:36:12,438 --> 00:36:14,868
- "The good"?
- Yes. The good.

543
00:36:14,973 --> 00:36:17,683
Well, Senator,

544
00:36:19,177 --> 00:36:21,907
the Greeks and Romans
provided...

545
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:24,523
a model of democracy,
which,

546
00:36:24,616 --> 00:36:27,386
I don't need
to tell you, the framers
of our own Constitution...

547
00:36:27,486 --> 00:36:29,686
used as their inspiration.

548
00:36:29,788 --> 00:36:34,658
But more to the point,
I think when the boys
read Plato, Aristotle,

549
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:37,360
Cicero, Julius Caesar even,

550
00:36:37,463 --> 00:36:39,633
they're put in
direct contact
with men who,

551
00:36:39,731 --> 00:36:41,631
in their own age,
exemplified...

552
00:36:41,733 --> 00:36:44,503
the highest standards
of statesmanship,

553
00:36:44,603 --> 00:36:46,513
of civic virtue,

554
00:36:46,605 --> 00:36:48,535
of character,
conviction--

555
00:36:48,640 --> 00:36:52,480
[ Chuckles ]
Boy, that is a horse
that can talk!

556
00:36:52,578 --> 00:36:55,948
So, you're sayin'
that my son Sedgewick
has his head up his ass.

557
00:36:56,047 --> 00:36:58,377
[ Chuckles ]

558
00:36:58,484 --> 00:37:01,754
Sir, it's my job
to mold your son's
character,

559
00:37:01,853 --> 00:37:03,793
and I think if--
Mold him?

560
00:37:03,889 --> 00:37:06,329
Jesus God in heaven, son.

561
00:37:06,425 --> 00:37:08,225
You're not gonna mold my boy.

562
00:37:08,327 --> 00:37:11,697
Your job is to teach my son.

563
00:37:11,797 --> 00:37:14,867
You teach him
his times tables.

564
00:37:14,966 --> 00:37:16,866
Teach him why
the world is round.

565
00:37:16,968 --> 00:37:19,538
Teach him who killed who
and when and where.

566
00:37:19,638 --> 00:37:22,008
That is your job.

567
00:37:23,742 --> 00:37:27,252
You, sir,
will not mold my son.

568
00:37:29,315 --> 00:37:32,875
I will mold him.

569
00:37:32,984 --> 00:37:35,994
- Yes, sir.
- Yes.

570
00:37:40,025 --> 00:37:41,785
Thanks for comin'.

571
00:37:43,194 --> 00:37:45,134
[ Players ]
Break!

572
00:37:45,230 --> 00:37:48,230
Marty. Marty.
Get back. Come on.

573
00:37:48,334 --> 00:37:51,474
Bell! Bell!

574
00:37:51,570 --> 00:37:54,170
- Uh, telephone for you.
- What?

575
00:37:54,272 --> 00:37:57,382
- It's a girl.
- [ Boys Razzing ]
Ohhh!

576
00:37:57,476 --> 00:38:00,446
[ Brewster ]
I'll bet it's that blond
from Saint Mary's.

577
00:38:00,546 --> 00:38:04,276
[ Excited Chatter ]
It's that redhead.

578
00:38:06,685 --> 00:38:09,755
[ Boys ]
Shhh.
Hello?

579
00:38:09,855 --> 00:38:13,755
[ Woman ]
Sedgewick?
Hold for your father.

580
00:38:13,859 --> 00:38:16,089
[ Sen. Bell ]
Let me ask you somethin'.
Do you know what...

581
00:38:16,194 --> 00:38:18,064
it's costing me to send
you to this place?

582
00:38:18,163 --> 00:38:20,533
- What's wrong?
- Shut up.

583
00:38:20,632 --> 00:38:25,602
My time is precious, son.
I thought I made that clear.

584
00:38:25,704 --> 00:38:28,074
I got better things to do
than to clean up after you.

585
00:38:28,173 --> 00:38:32,313
You understand me?
Yes, sir. Yes, sir.
I understand.

586
00:38:32,411 --> 00:38:34,851
Don't forget it.

587
00:38:41,820 --> 00:38:44,460
So, what did she say, man.
What did she say?

588
00:38:44,556 --> 00:38:47,126
Wrong girl.

589
00:38:47,225 --> 00:38:49,395
Wha-- What happened?
I don't know.

590
00:38:49,495 --> 00:38:52,525
[ Boy Shouting ]
Grey, wait up!

591
00:38:55,734 --> 00:38:59,044
[ Chattering, Laughter ]

592
00:39:16,455 --> 00:39:18,615
[ Knocking ]

593
00:39:22,193 --> 00:39:25,363
Deepak, would you give
Sedgewick and me a moment?
Yes, sir.

594
00:39:26,732 --> 00:39:28,672
Thank you.
You're welcome, sir.

595
00:39:30,301 --> 00:39:33,271
I spoke to your father.
I know.

596
00:39:33,371 --> 00:39:36,541
We had a real...
heart-to-heart.

597
00:39:41,146 --> 00:39:43,106
This is for you.

598
00:39:44,950 --> 00:39:48,450
Forgive the condition.
It was my textbook
in high school.

599
00:39:50,155 --> 00:39:51,985
It's quite good.

600
00:39:52,090 --> 00:39:55,130
The first chapter
has an outline...

601
00:39:55,226 --> 00:39:58,656
of all the material
which we'll be covering
this term.

602
00:39:58,764 --> 00:40:01,004
I thought
it might be helpful
in preparing...

603
00:40:01,099 --> 00:40:02,929
for the Mr. Julius Caesar
contest.

604
00:40:05,471 --> 00:40:08,671
The first quiz
is tomorrow morning.

605
00:40:08,774 --> 00:40:11,744
Look at chapter three--
"The Foundation
of the Republic."

606
00:40:14,112 --> 00:40:17,122
Sedgewick,
I'm lending you this book
because I believe in you.

607
00:40:19,317 --> 00:40:21,617
I think you could be
at the top of your class...

608
00:40:21,720 --> 00:40:23,620
if you wanted to be.

609
00:40:25,156 --> 00:40:28,426
It's entirely up to you.

610
00:41:08,233 --> 00:41:10,003
[ Hundert ]
Begin.

611
00:41:34,893 --> 00:41:36,763
Well done!

612
00:41:36,862 --> 00:41:39,632
[ Laughing ]
[ Bell Tolling ]

613
00:41:39,731 --> 00:41:41,601
Mr. Bell.

614
00:41:44,770 --> 00:41:46,770
You passed.

615
00:41:46,872 --> 00:41:49,472
It's only a C-minus.
[ Chuckles ]

616
00:41:49,575 --> 00:41:51,875
Well, you know what they say
about Rome.

617
00:41:51,977 --> 00:41:53,847
It wasn't
built in a day?

618
00:41:53,945 --> 00:41:55,845
No.
All roads lead to it.

619
00:41:55,947 --> 00:41:59,147
You were right
the first time.
Keep up the good work.

620
00:42:06,725 --> 00:42:08,585
Begin.

621
00:42:19,805 --> 00:42:22,765
[ Boy ]
Hey, Kevin!
How are you?

622
00:42:32,818 --> 00:42:34,988
Forty?
[ With Accent ]
Fourteen!

623
00:42:35,086 --> 00:42:37,786
Wh-Wh-Wh-What
are you saying?
Oh, 14.

624
00:42:37,889 --> 00:42:39,859
All right.
Alea jacta est.
What's that mean?

625
00:42:39,958 --> 00:42:41,958
"The die is cast."

626
00:42:42,060 --> 00:42:44,560
[ Chattering ]

627
00:42:46,431 --> 00:42:48,801
All right. All right.
Twenty. All right.
Next week then.

628
00:42:48,900 --> 00:42:51,100
I actually made the list.
Hey, look
who's up there.

629
00:42:53,371 --> 00:42:55,111
Good job.
Number five.
You're third.

630
00:43:09,621 --> 00:43:13,361
Miss Peters, no one
is going to look at this book
between now and 7:00 a.m.

631
00:43:13,458 --> 00:43:15,488
I understand that, Mr. Bell.
I understand.

632
00:43:15,593 --> 00:43:20,133
Every boy in the third forum
has asked to check
this book out overnight.

633
00:43:20,231 --> 00:43:22,431
Now, if I let you have it,
that wouldn't be fair, would it?

634
00:43:22,533 --> 00:43:24,943
It would be fair.
I know in my heart
it would be.

635
00:43:25,036 --> 00:43:28,206
Mr. Bell,
you are exasperating.
Miss Peters,

636
00:43:28,306 --> 00:43:30,376
that is
a great hairstyle.

637
00:43:30,475 --> 00:43:32,805
- Is that new?
- I've had it since 1958.

638
00:43:32,911 --> 00:43:36,251
Now, Mr. Bell,
in order to be fair
to all the students,

639
00:43:36,347 --> 00:43:39,517
it is important that
this book always be available
here at this desk.

640
00:43:39,617 --> 00:43:41,587
It is
Saint Benedict's policy.

641
00:43:41,687 --> 00:43:44,257
And it will be
on your desk
tomorrow morning.

642
00:43:44,355 --> 00:43:46,155
I promise you.
You have my word.

643
00:43:46,257 --> 00:43:48,987
No! No!

644
00:43:49,094 --> 00:43:51,904
Miss Peters, I can vouch
for this boy's character.

645
00:43:51,997 --> 00:43:53,897
He's one of my best students.

646
00:43:53,999 --> 00:43:56,499
If he says it will be
on your desk first thing
in the morning,

647
00:43:56,601 --> 00:43:59,501
it will be on your desk
first thing in the morning.

648
00:43:59,604 --> 00:44:02,074
I will, personally,
take full responsibility.

649
00:44:06,311 --> 00:44:08,151
Please.

650
00:44:10,716 --> 00:44:12,746
Thank you.
You're welcome.

651
00:44:12,851 --> 00:44:14,421
I wasn't kidding
about the hair.

652
00:44:14,519 --> 00:44:16,219
Thank you.
Good night.
Say thank you.

653
00:44:16,321 --> 00:44:18,921
Thank you.
Thanks.
Good evening.

654
00:44:19,024 --> 00:44:21,164
You run a tight ship,
Miss Peters.
Good night.

655
00:44:21,259 --> 00:44:25,099
- Good night!
Good night!
- I admire that--

656
00:44:28,433 --> 00:44:31,273
[ Louis ]
Come on, Sedge.
Come on.

657
00:44:31,369 --> 00:44:33,969
Yeah-yeah. Come on.
Pick it up. Pick it up.
Yeah!

658
00:44:34,072 --> 00:44:36,012
[ Louis ]
All right, Marty!

659
00:44:36,107 --> 00:44:38,677
[ Bell ]
Nice! Nice catch!

660
00:44:38,777 --> 00:44:41,177
[ Players' Shouting,
Chattering Continues ]

661
00:44:43,448 --> 00:44:47,288
Hey, Mr. Hundert!
Hey, you want to take
a couple of swings?

662
00:44:47,385 --> 00:44:49,515
[ Sedgewick ]
Come on, Mr. Hundert!

663
00:44:49,620 --> 00:44:52,160
- Aw, come on!
- Come on, Mr. Hundert!

664
00:44:52,257 --> 00:44:54,857
The library windows
are too close.
[ Boys Laughing ]

665
00:44:54,960 --> 00:44:57,530
Oh, yeah.
Right, Mr. Hundert.

666
00:44:57,628 --> 00:44:59,928
Hey, come on.
Show us how it's done,
old school.

667
00:45:00,031 --> 00:45:02,031
[ Boys ]
Ohhh!

668
00:45:02,133 --> 00:45:06,043
[ Brewster ]
Now you got to play,
Mr. Hundert. Come on!

669
00:45:06,137 --> 00:45:08,037
[ Chuckles ]

670
00:45:08,139 --> 00:45:10,309
"Old school," eh?
[ Louis ]
Uh-oh.

671
00:45:10,408 --> 00:45:12,638
Here we go.
Here comes the jacket.

672
00:45:12,744 --> 00:45:15,754
[ Chattering
Continues ]
Watch and learn.

673
00:45:17,348 --> 00:45:19,418
[ Boys Hooting ]

674
00:45:19,517 --> 00:45:21,847
Mr. Masoudi.
Mr. Hundert.

675
00:45:21,953 --> 00:45:24,363
It's been a while.
Yeah.
More like never.

676
00:45:24,455 --> 00:45:26,355
[ Boys Laughing ]

677
00:45:26,457 --> 00:45:29,687
Hey, back it up, everybody.
Come on. We got a hitter.
Move back.

678
00:45:29,795 --> 00:45:32,195
Back it up, fellows.
Back it up, outfield.

679
00:45:32,297 --> 00:45:34,127
Should've known.
[ Taps Bat On Book ]

680
00:45:35,967 --> 00:45:39,037
Egemus iaculatore,
non iacchi latore!

681
00:45:39,137 --> 00:45:43,067
- What does that mean?
- Means we want a pitcher,
not a glass of water.

682
00:45:43,174 --> 00:45:45,214
Oh, ho, ho!

683
00:45:45,310 --> 00:45:48,450
[ Field ] Come on, Sedgewick!
You got to get him back
for that one!

684
00:45:48,546 --> 00:45:51,216
- Right down the lane!
-[ Boys ] Ohhh!

685
00:45:51,316 --> 00:45:53,846
No batter.

686
00:45:59,324 --> 00:46:02,234
All right!
No batter! Come on!
Sedgewick, come on.

687
00:46:02,327 --> 00:46:05,797
[ Field ] Swing,
batter-batter-batter-batter!

688
00:46:22,113 --> 00:46:24,023
[ Sedgewick ]
Run!

689
00:46:24,115 --> 00:46:25,945
Run!

690
00:46:31,489 --> 00:46:33,859
Come on!
Hurry up!

691
00:46:33,959 --> 00:46:36,059
Move! Move!
Come on! Move it!
Let's go!

692
00:46:40,365 --> 00:46:42,095
Hey, come on!
Let's go to Brewster's room!

693
00:46:42,200 --> 00:46:44,040
Go, go, go, go, go, go!

694
00:46:49,875 --> 00:46:52,235
Shh. Shh.
Ooh. Ohh. Ohh.

695
00:46:53,478 --> 00:46:55,408
Nice hit, Mr. Hundert.

696
00:46:55,513 --> 00:46:58,883
Good, Mr. H.
"Old school"!
Shh.

697
00:46:58,984 --> 00:47:01,794
I suppose
you're as blind
as all of them.

698
00:47:01,887 --> 00:47:04,287
You have no idea how this came
to be hit through my window?
No idea.

699
00:47:04,389 --> 00:47:06,459
Oh, dear.
[ Woodbridge ]
No idea?

700
00:47:06,557 --> 00:47:09,957
- Ohhh, dear.
- You'll make
a good lawyer.

701
00:47:17,535 --> 00:47:20,005
It's 9:00.
You have until noon.

702
00:47:20,105 --> 00:47:22,965
This is the last of
the Mr. Julius Caesar
quizzes...

703
00:47:23,074 --> 00:47:25,414
and will do much
to determine
the three finalists.

704
00:47:25,510 --> 00:47:27,550
Good luck.

705
00:48:14,825 --> 00:48:16,655
[ Clock Ticking ]

706
00:48:32,377 --> 00:48:34,477
It's time, Mr. Bell.

707
00:48:40,085 --> 00:48:43,955
Mr. Bell,
I'm afraid you'll
have to stop.

708
00:48:53,431 --> 00:48:55,631
[ Sighs ]

709
00:49:59,564 --> 00:50:01,404
[ Exhales ]

710
00:50:58,123 --> 00:51:00,333
This is it,
Martin.

711
00:51:09,667 --> 00:51:11,897
[ Gleeful Laughter,
Overlapping Chatter ]

712
00:51:12,002 --> 00:51:15,312
- This is unbelievable!
- Oh, my God.
I got to call my father.

713
00:51:15,406 --> 00:51:17,336
[ Chattering ]

714
00:51:26,984 --> 00:51:29,194
[ Sighs ]

715
00:51:32,923 --> 00:51:34,933
All right. Let's go!

716
00:51:35,025 --> 00:51:37,655
[ Laughing, Shouting,
Indistinct ]

717
00:51:55,846 --> 00:51:57,776
Oh, I'm sorry.
I hope it's not too late.

718
00:51:57,882 --> 00:51:59,752
No. Not at all.
Not at all.

719
00:51:59,850 --> 00:52:01,950
How are you?
Uh.

720
00:52:02,052 --> 00:52:03,952
Well. Well.
I was just outside...

721
00:52:04,054 --> 00:52:05,994
observing
the wildlife.

722
00:52:06,090 --> 00:52:07,960
Sit down!
Would you like
your usual?

723
00:52:08,058 --> 00:52:10,088
No, thank you.
You sure?

724
00:52:10,195 --> 00:52:12,795
- Mm-hm.
- Do you mind if I, uh--

725
00:52:12,897 --> 00:52:15,027
How is
Mr. Julius Caesar going?

726
00:52:15,132 --> 00:52:19,402
Very-- Very well.
Even, uh, Sedgewick Bell.

727
00:52:19,504 --> 00:52:22,814
- Making progress?
- Oh, more than progress.

728
00:52:22,907 --> 00:52:26,107
He's-He's
come out of the darkness
into the light. It's--

729
00:52:26,211 --> 00:52:28,151
Please, sit down.

730
00:52:30,415 --> 00:52:33,315
[ Sighs ]
Cheers.

731
00:52:33,418 --> 00:52:35,388
I have to tell
you something.

732
00:52:35,486 --> 00:52:39,656
Tony's been offered
a lectureship at his
old college in Oxford.

733
00:52:39,757 --> 00:52:42,957
It's a good offer,
and he's accepted it.
[ Sighs ]

734
00:52:43,060 --> 00:52:45,230
I mean-- I mean to say
we've accepted it.

735
00:52:45,330 --> 00:52:48,270
Uh, we're-we're
going to England.

736
00:52:48,366 --> 00:52:50,836
W-W-When?

737
00:52:50,935 --> 00:52:52,865
At the end of the year?

738
00:52:52,970 --> 00:52:55,440
Well, if he wants
the position,

739
00:52:55,540 --> 00:52:58,280
he has to take it up
almost immediately,
and he wants the position.

740
00:52:58,376 --> 00:53:00,676
So, he's spoken
to Mr. Woodbridge,

741
00:53:00,778 --> 00:53:02,778
and he agreed.

742
00:53:05,916 --> 00:53:08,016
Just like that?

743
00:53:08,118 --> 00:53:09,888
Well, that's-- that's--

744
00:53:12,257 --> 00:53:16,157
That's remarkable, really.

745
00:53:16,261 --> 00:53:18,931
A lectureship, that's
a tremendous opportunity.

746
00:53:19,029 --> 00:53:21,869
Very good. Good.

747
00:53:30,675 --> 00:53:32,605
Well, you'll be missed.

748
00:53:33,911 --> 00:53:35,881
Both of you.

749
00:53:35,980 --> 00:53:39,320
I-I-I-- I should--
[ Chuckles ]

750
00:53:39,417 --> 00:53:41,687
Yes.

751
00:53:47,392 --> 00:53:49,992
[ Door Opens, Closes ]

752
00:53:57,502 --> 00:53:59,802
[ Grunting ]

753
00:54:05,443 --> 00:54:07,383
[ Applause ]

754
00:54:07,478 --> 00:54:09,748
[ Applause, Cheering ]

755
00:54:15,520 --> 00:54:17,490
Deepak!

756
00:54:17,588 --> 00:54:20,388
Yea, Louis!
Whoo!

757
00:54:24,962 --> 00:54:28,532
- Sedgewick!
- Yea, Louis!

758
00:54:32,937 --> 00:54:34,907
[ Both ]
Masoudi!

759
00:54:38,108 --> 00:54:40,108
Welcome--
Welcome, everyone,

760
00:54:40,210 --> 00:54:43,950
to the 73rd annual competition
of Mr. Julius Caesar.

761
00:54:44,048 --> 00:54:48,388
Our congratulations go
to Messrs. Louis Masoudi,

762
00:54:48,486 --> 00:54:50,416
[ Cheers, Applause ]

763
00:54:50,521 --> 00:54:53,791
Deepak Mehta...
[ Boys ]
Deepak!

764
00:54:53,891 --> 00:54:55,891
[ Applause Continue ]
and Sedgewick Bell.

765
00:55:03,834 --> 00:55:06,804
Of course, this competition,
this year's event,

766
00:55:06,904 --> 00:55:09,474
would not have been
possible if it hadn't been
for the passion...

767
00:55:09,574 --> 00:55:11,714
of our moderator.

768
00:55:11,809 --> 00:55:16,009
I give you
our learned Mr. Hundert.

769
00:55:16,113 --> 00:55:18,453
[ Boy ]
Mr. H.!

770
00:55:23,521 --> 00:55:26,661
Headmaster.
Gentlemen.

771
00:55:26,757 --> 00:55:29,557
Ladies.
Distinguished guests.

772
00:55:29,660 --> 00:55:32,560
Questions will be posed
to our three young scholars...

773
00:55:32,663 --> 00:55:34,573
in increasing levels
of difficulty.

774
00:55:34,665 --> 00:55:36,925
I ask you all to please
hold your applause...

775
00:55:37,034 --> 00:55:38,904
till the end
of each round.

776
00:55:39,003 --> 00:55:41,543
May the best man win.

777
00:55:45,142 --> 00:55:47,112
Mr. Masoudi,

778
00:55:47,211 --> 00:55:49,681
which emperor
sought to return...

779
00:55:49,780 --> 00:55:51,720
all power to the Senate,

780
00:55:51,816 --> 00:55:54,116
only to garner
even greater power?

781
00:55:54,218 --> 00:55:56,488
Augustus, r-right?

782
00:55:56,587 --> 00:55:58,487
Correct.
Oh.

783
00:55:58,589 --> 00:56:00,489
Mr. Mehta,

784
00:56:00,591 --> 00:56:03,791
who introduced
the professional army to Rome?

785
00:56:03,894 --> 00:56:07,434
Gaius Marius, sir,
in 104 B.C.
Correct.

786
00:56:07,532 --> 00:56:09,902
Mr. Bell,

787
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:12,140
on which Roman hill...

788
00:56:12,236 --> 00:56:14,936
was the infamous Tarpeian Rock?

789
00:56:22,279 --> 00:56:24,149
[ Coughs ]

790
00:56:31,656 --> 00:56:33,456
[ Whispering ]
It's Capitoline.

791
00:56:37,495 --> 00:56:40,565
Uh, Capitoline?

792
00:56:41,732 --> 00:56:43,832
Correct.

793
00:56:49,139 --> 00:56:52,909
Mr. Masoudi,
what is the full name
of the author of the Georgics?

794
00:56:53,010 --> 00:56:55,580
Publius Vergilius Maro.

795
00:56:55,680 --> 00:56:57,750
Military conquest
of 106 A.D. involved
the bridging of the Danube?

796
00:56:57,848 --> 00:56:59,578
Trajan conquers Dacia.
Correct.

797
00:56:59,684 --> 00:57:02,224
What Roman Emperor might
the Greeks have characterized
"philosopher king"?

798
00:57:02,319 --> 00:57:05,689
- Marcus Aurelius.
- Correct.

799
00:57:08,859 --> 00:57:10,759
[ Boy ]
All right, Sedgewick!

800
00:57:10,861 --> 00:57:14,501
Quiet.

801
00:57:14,599 --> 00:57:17,699
Quiet, please.

802
00:57:18,803 --> 00:57:20,573
Mr. Masoudi--

803
00:57:22,272 --> 00:57:24,482
Mr. Masoudi, who were
the first emperors...

804
00:57:24,575 --> 00:57:27,305
to rule over
the divided empire?

805
00:57:27,411 --> 00:57:31,221
Valentinian I
and Caecina.

806
00:57:31,315 --> 00:57:33,445
I'm sorry,
that is incorrect.

807
00:57:33,551 --> 00:57:35,851
[ Crowd Groans ]

808
00:57:35,953 --> 00:57:37,993
Mr. Mehta?

809
00:57:38,088 --> 00:57:41,458
Valentinian I
and Valens.

810
00:57:41,559 --> 00:57:43,629
Correct.
Thank you, Mr. Masoudi.
[ Applause ]

811
00:57:43,728 --> 00:57:46,658
You may sit down.

812
00:57:46,764 --> 00:57:49,604
[ Hundert ] Well done.
[ Boy ]
Good job, man.

813
00:57:56,040 --> 00:57:59,280
Mr. Bell, which general...

814
00:57:59,376 --> 00:58:01,606
had the support
of the aristocrats...

815
00:58:01,712 --> 00:58:05,122
in the civil war
of 88 B.C.?

816
00:58:31,008 --> 00:58:32,608
[ Whispering ]
Sulla.

817
00:58:35,245 --> 00:58:37,275
Lucius Cornelius Sulla.

818
00:58:42,720 --> 00:58:45,560
That is correct.

819
00:58:45,656 --> 00:58:49,056
That's my boy!

820
00:58:52,663 --> 00:58:55,533
Mr. Woodbridge, I think
Sedgewick Bell is cheating.

821
00:58:57,001 --> 00:58:58,901
Let it go.
What?

822
00:58:59,003 --> 00:59:01,913
Ignore it.

823
00:59:02,006 --> 00:59:04,636
Ignore it?
Ignore it.

824
00:59:19,156 --> 00:59:23,956
Mr. Mehta, into how many
administrative regions
did Augustus divide Rome?

825
00:59:40,144 --> 00:59:42,884
Fourteen.

826
00:59:42,980 --> 00:59:45,250
Excuse me?
How many?

827
00:59:45,349 --> 00:59:47,379
- Fourteen.
- That is correct.

828
00:59:47,484 --> 00:59:50,824
[ Boy ]
All right, Deepak! Whoo!
[ Applause ]

829
01:00:05,235 --> 01:00:07,595
Mr. Bell,

830
01:00:09,206 --> 01:00:11,636
who was Hamilcar Barca?

831
01:00:24,354 --> 01:00:27,194
Would you repeat
the question, please?

832
01:00:27,291 --> 01:00:30,961
Who was Hamilcar Barca?

833
01:00:45,109 --> 01:00:47,779
I don't know.
[ Crowd Groaning ]

834
01:00:58,355 --> 01:01:00,685
Mr. Mehta,
who was Hamilcar Barca?

835
01:01:00,791 --> 01:01:05,761
The Carthaginian general
victorious in the battle
of Drepana in 249.

836
01:01:05,863 --> 01:01:09,973
An unequaled commander
with the misfortune
of being on the wrong side.

837
01:01:10,067 --> 01:01:12,837
That is correct.

838
01:01:12,937 --> 01:01:15,407
[ Applause, Cheers ]

839
01:01:29,787 --> 01:01:32,017
Thank you, Louis.
Thank you.

840
01:01:40,630 --> 01:01:42,430
Mr. Julius Caesar.

841
01:01:59,383 --> 01:02:02,193
Harrison, how's your sister?
You better not
speak to her.

842
01:02:02,286 --> 01:02:05,016
Mr. H., good job, man.
Good job, Mr. Hundert.

843
01:02:06,356 --> 01:02:08,226
[ Boy ]
Hey, Jeremy!
How you doin'?

844
01:02:10,995 --> 01:02:13,155
[ Knocking ]
[ Door Opening ]

845
01:02:18,869 --> 01:02:21,009
[ Door Closing ]

846
01:02:23,473 --> 01:02:27,013
That was quite
an interesting performance
this afternoon.

847
01:02:27,111 --> 01:02:29,151
I knew you saw.

848
01:02:44,661 --> 01:02:48,531
Why, Sedgewick?

849
01:02:48,632 --> 01:02:50,732
You knew the material.

850
01:02:50,835 --> 01:02:53,465
Why not?

851
01:02:59,009 --> 01:03:02,009
How come you didn't stand up
and call me out?

852
01:03:03,280 --> 01:03:06,120
It's a complicated matter,
Sedgewick.

853
01:03:08,685 --> 01:03:11,085
It wasn't because
of my father, was it?

854
01:03:13,991 --> 01:03:16,461
It had nothing to do
with your father.

855
01:03:20,530 --> 01:03:24,330
Sure, Mr. Hundert.
Sure.

856
01:03:48,458 --> 01:03:51,298
[ Door Closing ]

857
01:03:51,395 --> 01:03:54,125
William!

858
01:03:54,231 --> 01:03:57,231
I came by
to congratulate you.
That was terrific!

859
01:03:57,334 --> 01:04:00,604
Sedgewick Bell--
quite the surprise.
Yes.

860
01:04:00,704 --> 01:04:04,044
He's come a long way.
You must be very proud of him.

861
01:04:04,141 --> 01:04:06,141
It makes
for wonderful theater.

862
01:04:06,243 --> 01:04:09,783
In fact, I was thinking,
I might make the suggestion
to you, and to Mr. Woodbridge,

863
01:04:09,880 --> 01:04:11,780
that in the future
more alumni are invited.

864
01:04:11,882 --> 01:04:15,992
Not just the parents
of the finalists,
but maybe all of the alumni.

865
01:04:16,086 --> 01:04:18,386
You know?
And instead of it happening
just on any old weekday,

866
01:04:18,488 --> 01:04:21,428
think about making it
a weekend event.

867
01:04:21,525 --> 01:04:24,655
You know, for fund-raising,
which would augur well
for your headmastership.

868
01:04:24,761 --> 01:04:29,001
Mmm.
What's wrong?

869
01:04:29,099 --> 01:04:30,999
[ Sighs ]

870
01:04:33,637 --> 01:04:36,907
Sedgewick cheated.

871
01:04:37,007 --> 01:04:38,907
What?
Come in.

872
01:04:41,645 --> 01:04:44,705
Well, the pressure to succeed
can be oppressive.

873
01:04:44,814 --> 01:04:46,924
And, right or wrong,

874
01:04:47,017 --> 01:04:50,517
Woodbridge felt it was in the
best interests of the school's
endowment to let it pass.

875
01:04:50,620 --> 01:04:53,260
Come on. William, you didn't
put the boy up on the stage.

876
01:04:53,357 --> 01:04:55,257
He got there
on his own merit.

877
01:04:55,359 --> 01:04:57,329
All you did was inspire him...

878
01:04:57,427 --> 01:05:00,997
to study and to learn,
and for that
you should be commended.

879
01:05:18,983 --> 01:05:21,923
And thus began
an uneasy truce...

880
01:05:22,019 --> 01:05:24,849
between Sedgewick Bell
and myself.

881
01:05:24,955 --> 01:05:28,285
Whatever spark had ignited him
during the previous months...

882
01:05:28,392 --> 01:05:30,492
was now extinguished.

883
01:05:30,594 --> 01:05:32,934
And his brief flirtation
with diligence...

884
01:05:33,030 --> 01:05:35,930
was supplanted by
a renewed appetite
for brashness,

885
01:05:36,033 --> 01:05:38,403
contempt and folly.

886
01:05:40,070 --> 01:05:44,840
As for his classmates,
his effect on them
was almost hypnotic.

887
01:05:44,941 --> 01:05:46,881
Wherever he went,
they hovered,

888
01:05:46,977 --> 01:05:49,347
Sedgewick for class president.
utterly blind to any
deficiencies in his character.

889
01:05:49,446 --> 01:05:51,476
Food fight!

890
01:05:51,581 --> 01:05:53,851
So, his remaining years at
Saint Benedict's passed...

891
01:05:53,950 --> 01:05:56,920
amidst an inexhaustible series
of pranks,

892
01:05:57,021 --> 01:05:59,421
and an avalanche
of C's and D's.

893
01:05:59,523 --> 01:06:02,233
Sedgewick Hyram Bell.

894
01:06:02,326 --> 01:06:05,026
And though
his father's influence
guaranteed him a place at Yale,

895
01:06:06,997 --> 01:06:09,297
it was with a
profound sense of failure,

896
01:06:09,399 --> 01:06:12,799
that in the spring of 1976,

897
01:06:12,902 --> 01:06:17,412
I handed
Sedgewick Bell his diploma.

898
01:06:28,552 --> 01:06:30,652
[ Bell Tolling ]

899
01:06:42,932 --> 01:06:45,342
According to Heraclitus,

900
01:06:45,435 --> 01:06:48,035
we cannot step into
the same river twice.

901
01:06:48,138 --> 01:06:50,138
"In the flow of time,"
he argued,

902
01:06:50,240 --> 01:06:53,510
"an opportunity lost
is lost forever."

903
01:06:56,880 --> 01:07:01,220
And yet, when Elizabeth's
marriage ended and she
returned home from England,

904
01:07:01,318 --> 01:07:03,648
the waters in which
we found ourselves swimming...

905
01:07:03,753 --> 01:07:09,563
were precisely as lovely
as those we had
earlier only imagined.

906
01:07:09,659 --> 01:07:12,059
But if time had made
concessions for love,

907
01:07:12,162 --> 01:07:14,562
it made none for death.

908
01:07:14,664 --> 01:07:17,334
And when our headmaster,
Terence Woodbridge,
passed away,

909
01:07:17,434 --> 01:07:19,944
I felt profound sadness.

910
01:07:20,036 --> 01:07:23,636
Though the incident
with Sedgewick Bell
had tested our friendship,

911
01:07:23,740 --> 01:07:25,780
he had for half a century...

912
01:07:25,875 --> 01:07:30,805
devoted himself
to St. Benedict's tradition of
academic and moral leadership--

913
01:07:30,914 --> 01:07:33,784
A tradition, I realized,
that was now mine...

914
01:07:33,883 --> 01:07:35,793
to safeguard.

915
01:07:55,739 --> 01:07:57,609
Good luck, Mr. Hundert.

916
01:07:57,707 --> 01:08:00,177
Thank you, Mr. Horner.

917
01:08:01,511 --> 01:08:04,081
Everyone.
Welcome.

918
01:08:04,181 --> 01:08:06,451
Larry, good to see you.

919
01:08:06,550 --> 01:08:10,550
Hi.
Mr. Phillips.

920
01:08:10,654 --> 01:08:12,724
Mr. Hundert.
Good to see you again.

921
01:08:12,822 --> 01:08:14,862
Uh, please take a seat.

922
01:08:14,958 --> 01:08:17,088
Uh--
Let's get right to it,
shall we?

923
01:08:17,194 --> 01:08:21,064
As you wish.

924
01:08:21,165 --> 01:08:24,725
Mr. Hundert, your contributions
at Saint Benedict's
have been extraordinary.

925
01:08:24,834 --> 01:08:28,214
Your tenure is unmatched,
and you are loved
and revered by all.

926
01:08:28,305 --> 01:08:31,375
The notion of taking you away
from your classroom...

927
01:08:31,475 --> 01:08:33,735
is one to which
we've given great thought.

928
01:08:33,843 --> 01:08:36,053
A headmaster's job
is a rigorous one.

929
01:08:36,146 --> 01:08:39,276
The travel demands
and meeting schedules
have become unending.

930
01:08:39,383 --> 01:08:43,123
One must be prepared
to do battle 24 hours a day
and sometimes longer.

931
01:08:43,220 --> 01:08:46,490
Well, having rowed our lake
every day, weather permitting,

932
01:08:46,590 --> 01:08:49,330
through the last seven
presidential administrations,

933
01:08:49,426 --> 01:08:51,726
I feel more than ready
to do battle.

934
01:08:56,099 --> 01:08:58,299
What we're saying,
Mr. Hundert, is--

935
01:09:00,837 --> 01:09:03,807
Mr. Hundert,
what experience do you have...

936
01:09:03,907 --> 01:09:07,537
with fund-raising
or building endowments?

937
01:09:07,644 --> 01:09:10,714
Mr. Hundert, we can't afford
to lose you as a teacher here.

938
01:09:10,814 --> 01:09:13,654
The reality is this.
Enrollment is down.

939
01:09:13,750 --> 01:09:16,590
People are thinking
of Saint Benedict's
as old-fashioned.

940
01:09:16,686 --> 01:09:20,716
We need to look
more to the future
and less to the past.

941
01:09:25,962 --> 01:09:29,232
Then who?

942
01:09:30,567 --> 01:09:32,737
Someone from the outside?

943
01:09:32,836 --> 01:09:35,336
James Ellerby.

944
01:09:37,441 --> 01:09:39,341
Excuse me?

945
01:09:39,443 --> 01:09:42,253
You did realize he made
application for the job.

946
01:09:42,346 --> 01:09:44,746
Mr. Ellerby has proposed
some very exciting...
No.

947
01:09:44,848 --> 01:09:48,288
fund-raising possibilities.

948
01:09:51,621 --> 01:09:54,761
Miss Johnston, I've been
assistant headmaster
for 17 years.

949
01:09:54,858 --> 01:09:56,828
I fail to see how
Mr. Ellerby's...

950
01:09:56,926 --> 01:09:58,896
qualifications
as a fund-raiser--

951
01:09:58,995 --> 01:10:00,825
It's not just
about fund-raising.

952
01:10:00,930 --> 01:10:04,500
James Ellerby is
a forward-thinking man.

953
01:10:04,601 --> 01:10:06,501
He's reached out
to the community.

954
01:10:06,603 --> 01:10:09,873
He's built relationships
to some of the most
important alumni.

955
01:10:09,973 --> 01:10:12,413
He is a wonderful communicator,

956
01:10:12,509 --> 01:10:14,409
with impeccable
moral standing...

957
01:10:14,511 --> 01:10:17,611
and an unwavering
commitment to this school.

958
01:10:18,848 --> 01:10:20,678
So--

959
01:10:23,152 --> 01:10:25,492
Then you leave me no choice
but to tender my resignation.

960
01:10:25,589 --> 01:10:29,329
I would ask you not to.

961
01:10:29,426 --> 01:10:31,826
You are a teacher, and
the finest one we've ever had.

962
01:10:33,997 --> 01:10:35,997
Please excuse me.

963
01:10:36,099 --> 01:10:40,199
Please understand, Mr. Hundert,
you will be welcome back here
at Saint Benedict's at any time.

964
01:10:40,304 --> 01:10:42,914
Th-The door
will forever be open.

965
01:11:37,226 --> 01:11:39,326
I'm still shocked
at James Ellerby.

966
01:11:39,429 --> 01:11:43,829
Oh, I'm not.
I'm only shocked
that I didn't see it coming.

967
01:11:43,933 --> 01:11:48,103
I realize now he'd been
conducting a behind-the-scenes
campaign for years.

968
01:11:48,204 --> 01:11:53,044
He's godfather to two
of the board members' children,
for God's sake.

969
01:11:53,142 --> 01:11:55,312
[ Sighs ]

970
01:11:55,412 --> 01:11:57,852
I have...

971
01:11:57,947 --> 01:12:00,277
so many other things...

972
01:12:00,384 --> 01:12:02,694
that I've always
wanted to do.

973
01:12:02,786 --> 01:12:04,616
I-I've always
wanted to write.

974
01:12:04,721 --> 01:12:08,091
I have an idea
for a book on the early days
of the Republic.

975
01:12:08,191 --> 01:12:11,031
Well,

976
01:12:11,127 --> 01:12:13,057
here's to Saint Benedict's.

977
01:12:27,677 --> 01:12:29,807
I brought you some tea.

978
01:12:29,913 --> 01:12:31,883
Mmm.

979
01:12:31,981 --> 01:12:33,851
Going well?

980
01:12:33,950 --> 01:12:36,590
Oh, it's virtually
writing itself.

981
01:12:36,686 --> 01:12:39,686
Good.
Then I won't interrupt you.

982
01:12:39,789 --> 01:12:43,059
Carry on, then.
Good writing.

983
01:12:53,002 --> 01:12:54,842
[ Sighs ]

984
01:13:17,126 --> 01:13:20,156
William?

985
01:13:20,263 --> 01:13:22,903
James Ellerby's on the phone.

986
01:13:22,999 --> 01:13:27,899
The merger makes him C.E.O.
of one of the largest
corporations in America.

987
01:13:28,004 --> 01:13:32,084
But what does any of this
have to do with me?

988
01:13:32,175 --> 01:13:34,275
Here's the thing.
He's informed us
that he would like to make...

989
01:13:34,377 --> 01:13:36,647
a substantial donation
to Saint Benedict's...

990
01:13:36,746 --> 01:13:38,646
with one condition.

991
01:13:38,748 --> 01:13:42,088
He would like to hold
a rematch of the
Mr. Julius Caesar contest.

992
01:13:42,185 --> 01:13:44,485
A rematch?

993
01:13:44,588 --> 01:13:46,718
With you
as master of ceremonies.

994
01:13:46,823 --> 01:13:49,193
Why?

995
01:13:49,292 --> 01:13:51,192
He says he would like to
reclaim his intellectual honor.

996
01:13:51,294 --> 01:13:55,164
And he says his secretary's
already located Louis Masoudi
and Deepak Mehta...

997
01:13:55,264 --> 01:13:58,074
and doesn't really
anticipate too much trouble
tracking down the others.

998
01:13:58,167 --> 01:14:00,667
Didn't you tell him
I had retired?

999
01:14:00,770 --> 01:14:03,540
Well, he asked
for you and only you.

1000
01:14:03,640 --> 01:14:06,110
He insisted on it
as a condition.

1001
01:14:06,209 --> 01:14:12,049
And did Mr. Bell say how much
of a donation he was planning
to make to Saint Benedict's?

1002
01:14:12,148 --> 01:14:15,818
Enough for a 25,000-square-foot
addition to the library...

1003
01:14:15,919 --> 01:14:17,849
in honor
of his father.

1004
01:14:17,954 --> 01:14:20,864
It would be the school's
single largest donation to date.

1005
01:14:20,957 --> 01:14:26,697
And a retired history professor
with no fund-raising ability
is the linchpin.

1006
01:14:26,796 --> 01:14:29,526
Yes.

1007
01:14:29,633 --> 01:14:31,973
It's ironic.

1008
01:14:32,068 --> 01:14:34,138
Life's a funny thing,
William, huh?

1009
01:14:34,237 --> 01:14:36,867
Where does he propose
to hold this rematch?

1010
01:14:36,973 --> 01:14:40,683
[ Ellerby ] A resort
on the Gold Coast of Long Island
that his company owns.

1011
01:14:40,777 --> 01:14:42,647
The proposed itinerary
is all in here.

1012
01:15:03,099 --> 01:15:05,229
[ Helicopter Whirring ]

1013
01:15:35,431 --> 01:15:37,331
Ah, Mr. Hundert.

1014
01:15:37,433 --> 01:15:39,543
Mr. Bell.

1015
01:15:39,636 --> 01:15:41,836
And how was your trip?

1016
01:15:41,938 --> 01:15:43,838
It was unlike anything
I've ever experienced.

1017
01:15:43,940 --> 01:15:47,140
I now know how Caesar Augustus
must have felt...

1018
01:15:47,243 --> 01:15:49,853
being carried head high
in the litter past the Tiber.
Oh, really?

1019
01:15:49,946 --> 01:15:52,746
It's all a bit overwhelming.

1020
01:15:52,849 --> 01:15:56,219
Well, don't let this fool you.
I am still the son
of a pig farmer.

1021
01:15:56,319 --> 01:16:00,689
I really appreciate
you coming here this weekend.

1022
01:16:00,790 --> 01:16:03,830
You know, you think things
that happen when you're 15
don't matter, but--

1023
01:16:16,272 --> 01:16:18,112
[ Chattering ]

1024
01:16:24,013 --> 01:16:27,423
[ Man ]
Great party.
I'm so glad to see you.

1025
01:16:27,516 --> 01:16:30,316
[ Bell ] I mean, this guy--
you should have saw him
in boarding school.

1026
01:16:30,419 --> 01:16:32,589
I mean, oh--
Excuse me, guys,
I'll be right back.

1027
01:16:33,990 --> 01:16:35,890
Mr. Hundert. Please.

1028
01:16:35,992 --> 01:16:38,232
Sedgewick.
Please come and join us.

1029
01:16:38,327 --> 01:16:40,957
I'd like you to meet
my top two advisors.
This is John.

1030
01:16:41,064 --> 01:16:42,974
Hi, John.
Hi, Mr. Hundert.

1031
01:16:43,066 --> 01:16:45,026
- And Robert.
- How do you do,
Mr. Hundert?

1032
01:16:45,134 --> 01:16:49,244
- Very well, thank you.
- And, my lovely wife, Victoria.

1033
01:16:49,338 --> 01:16:53,108
So nice
to finally meet you, sir.
I've heard so much about you.

1034
01:16:53,209 --> 01:16:56,409
All right, all right!

1035
01:16:56,512 --> 01:16:58,452
[ Bell ]
This is the time
of the evening...

1036
01:16:58,547 --> 01:17:01,177
where I'd like to
call upon a unique g--

1037
01:17:01,284 --> 01:17:04,224
Oh, Brewster.

1038
01:17:06,022 --> 01:17:08,162
I'd like to call upon
a unique gentleman,

1039
01:17:08,257 --> 01:17:12,427
one who managed to chisel
the rise and fall of
the Roman Empire into our minds,

1040
01:17:12,528 --> 01:17:15,558
whether we liked
it or not.

1041
01:17:15,665 --> 01:17:18,395
- Our Mr. Hundert.
- [ Man ]
Hear! Hear!

1042
01:17:18,501 --> 01:17:20,741
[ Applause ]

1043
01:17:24,507 --> 01:17:28,007
I must say, um--
You've all grown up.

1044
01:17:28,111 --> 01:17:31,511
I feel as if
I should do roll call...

1045
01:17:31,614 --> 01:17:33,384
just to be sure
to whom I'm speaking.

1046
01:17:33,482 --> 01:17:37,022
Seriously,
to stand here among...

1047
01:17:37,120 --> 01:17:39,260
my students--

1048
01:17:39,355 --> 01:17:41,285
[ Laughing ]

1049
01:17:45,594 --> 01:17:47,534
I expect to see
you all in detention.

1050
01:17:47,630 --> 01:17:49,430
[ Together ]
Oh, whoa-whoa-whoa-whoa-whoa!

1051
01:17:49,532 --> 01:17:52,272
Now, risen to become...

1052
01:17:52,368 --> 01:17:55,768
a bunch
of drunken reprobates...

1053
01:17:57,741 --> 01:18:01,081
who are now a veritable
who's who of American industry,

1054
01:18:01,177 --> 01:18:04,077
law, finance
and higher education.

1055
01:18:04,180 --> 01:18:08,380
Well, I can think
of few greater privileges.

1056
01:18:08,484 --> 01:18:10,394
I thank you.

1057
01:18:10,486 --> 01:18:13,116
[ Laughs ]

1058
01:18:14,657 --> 01:18:17,187
More coffee, sir?
Yes. Thank you.

1059
01:18:21,865 --> 01:18:25,095
Mr. Hundert?

1060
01:18:28,137 --> 01:18:33,007
Martin. Martin Blythe.

1061
01:18:33,109 --> 01:18:35,779
It's good
to see you again, sir.

1062
01:18:35,879 --> 01:18:39,079
When you weren't
at the dinner last night,
I was worried that perhaps--

1063
01:18:39,182 --> 01:18:41,282
That I wasn't
gonna make it?

1064
01:18:41,384 --> 01:18:43,754
Oh, come on, Mr. Hundert.
I wouldn't miss this.

1065
01:18:43,853 --> 01:18:45,763
You know, I was going
over my notes the other day...

1066
01:18:45,855 --> 01:18:48,185
in preparation
for the--
The competition.

1067
01:18:48,291 --> 01:18:51,161
Yes, and I-I came
across an essay...

1068
01:18:51,260 --> 01:18:55,360
that you once wrote
on the Five Good Emperors.

1069
01:18:55,464 --> 01:18:57,374
It's amazing.
You were--

1070
01:18:57,466 --> 01:18:59,696
You were always
a wonderful student, Martin.

1071
01:18:59,803 --> 01:19:01,703
I, uh,

1072
01:19:01,805 --> 01:19:05,235
I never quite
gave you your due.

1073
01:19:05,341 --> 01:19:08,181
Now, why would
you say that?

1074
01:19:08,277 --> 01:19:11,707
Oh, I don't know, I--

1075
01:19:11,815 --> 01:19:14,875
My God, Mr. Hundert,
you--

1076
01:19:14,984 --> 01:19:19,024
That recommendation you wrote
when I applied for the Academy--

1077
01:19:19,122 --> 01:19:23,162
It was glowing.
It was--

1078
01:19:23,259 --> 01:19:26,199
Of course
you gave me my due.

1079
01:19:26,295 --> 01:19:28,195
You gave me
more than my due.

1080
01:19:29,365 --> 01:19:31,265
[ Shouting, Indistinct ]

1081
01:19:31,367 --> 01:19:34,067
Grab his wrists! Push.

1082
01:19:34,170 --> 01:19:36,310
Okay, Deepak. With what emperor
did the Julio-Claudian dynasty
come to an end?

1083
01:19:36,405 --> 01:19:38,235
Mmm.
Masoudi?

1084
01:19:38,341 --> 01:19:40,741
Nero.

1085
01:19:40,844 --> 01:19:45,254
We did it! Yeah!
Good job, Robert.
All right, all right.

1086
01:19:45,348 --> 01:19:47,278
Time to study.

1087
01:19:47,383 --> 01:19:51,023
No. Come on, Dad!
One more game?

1088
01:19:57,693 --> 01:19:59,833
[ Knocking ]

1089
01:19:59,929 --> 01:20:02,469
Come in.

1090
01:20:02,565 --> 01:20:05,265
- Sorry to bother you, sir.
- Oh, no bother.

1091
01:20:05,368 --> 01:20:07,268
How are you, Sedgewick?
I am fine.

1092
01:20:07,370 --> 01:20:10,410
I have something for you.

1093
01:20:10,506 --> 01:20:12,576
Oh, my God.

1094
01:20:12,675 --> 01:20:16,305
I hope there's
no late fee.

1095
01:20:16,412 --> 01:20:20,082
I understand now
why it meant so much to you.

1096
01:20:21,650 --> 01:20:25,720
"The Greeks carve cold marble
until it almost comes to life.

1097
01:20:25,821 --> 01:20:28,661
"The Greeks compose
great orations...

1098
01:20:28,757 --> 01:20:31,627
"and measure the heavens so well
they can predict the rising
of the stars.

1099
01:20:31,727 --> 01:20:34,757
"But you, Roman,
remember your great arts.

1100
01:20:34,864 --> 01:20:37,204
"To govern the people
with authority...

1101
01:20:37,300 --> 01:20:39,800
"to establish peace
under the rule of law.

1102
01:20:39,903 --> 01:20:44,043
To conquer the mighty,
and to show them mercy
once they are conquered."

1103
01:20:50,579 --> 01:20:54,119
Well, it's nice to see
you haven't marked it up.
[ Chuckles ]

1104
01:20:55,684 --> 01:20:58,194
Yeah, I had my moments,
didn't I?

1105
01:20:58,287 --> 01:21:01,317
Your years, you mean.

1106
01:21:01,424 --> 01:21:04,934
Why did you
put up with me?

1107
01:21:06,963 --> 01:21:09,073
I suppose because...

1108
01:21:09,165 --> 01:21:13,865
I saw a young man
who was under
a great deal of pressure.

1109
01:21:13,970 --> 01:21:16,670
And, not unlike your upbringing,

1110
01:21:16,772 --> 01:21:21,142
I too had a-- a father
who was a very busy man.

1111
01:21:22,378 --> 01:21:25,808
[ Chuckles ]
You know, it's funny.

1112
01:21:25,915 --> 01:21:28,545
I remember traveling
with my father,

1113
01:21:28,651 --> 01:21:30,821
and there wasn't a person
he didn't know.

1114
01:21:30,920 --> 01:21:33,760
He talked,
and he told stories,
and he was,

1115
01:21:33,856 --> 01:21:37,586
he was always full of advice,
always charming, and always...

1116
01:21:37,693 --> 01:21:39,663
the Senator.

1117
01:21:39,762 --> 01:21:43,572
But I can hardly remember
having a real conversation
with him.

1118
01:21:43,666 --> 01:21:46,396
In fact, I wonder if he ever
heard a word I'd spoken.

1119
01:21:49,105 --> 01:21:53,735
Years ago, I, uh,

1120
01:21:53,842 --> 01:21:56,982
visited him in the hospital
and he was very, very sick.

1121
01:21:57,080 --> 01:22:00,580
And it took a moment for him
to realize I was at his bedside.

1122
01:22:02,986 --> 01:22:06,386
And he began to cry.

1123
01:22:06,489 --> 01:22:08,519
And through his tears,
he looked up at me and he said,

1124
01:22:13,029 --> 01:22:15,529
"Talk to me."

1125
01:22:18,434 --> 01:22:21,404
[ Sighs ]

1126
01:22:21,504 --> 01:22:24,414
I wondered where to begin.
I had no idea.

1127
01:22:25,808 --> 01:22:29,578
But at the very moment
I started to talk,

1128
01:22:30,946 --> 01:22:35,016
he closed his eyes
and he passed away...

1129
01:22:36,619 --> 01:22:41,189
without having heard a word.

1130
01:22:44,060 --> 01:22:45,960
Ah, that son of a bitch.

1131
01:22:52,168 --> 01:22:54,768
Oh, God. Thanks.
Thanks again, Mr. Hundert,

1132
01:22:54,870 --> 01:22:58,070
for, uh, for making
all this possible.

1133
01:22:58,174 --> 01:23:00,144
It is my extreme pleasure.

1134
01:23:00,243 --> 01:23:02,383
I'm looking forward
to this evening.
Best of luck.

1135
01:23:02,478 --> 01:23:05,608
Oh, much obliged.
I'll need all the help
I can get.

1136
01:23:05,714 --> 01:23:07,724
I don't think so.

1137
01:23:07,816 --> 01:23:10,346
See ya downstairs.
I'll see you downstairs.

1138
01:23:17,726 --> 01:23:19,656
[ Applause ]

1139
01:23:23,432 --> 01:23:25,302
[ Man ]
Go, Deepak!

1140
01:23:27,236 --> 01:23:29,266
[ Man ]
All right, Sedgewick!

1141
01:23:42,551 --> 01:23:45,491
It's not a good time.
I've gotta call you right back.
Thank you. Bye.

1142
01:23:45,588 --> 01:23:47,518
Sorry, sir.

1143
01:23:47,623 --> 01:23:50,293
Were you talking
to your broker?

1144
01:23:54,297 --> 01:23:56,327
Good evening,
ladies and gentlemen.

1145
01:23:56,432 --> 01:23:59,302
Our three scholars
will be posed questions...

1146
01:23:59,402 --> 01:24:02,312
on ancient history
in increasing levels
of difficulty.

1147
01:24:02,405 --> 01:24:05,865
I ask you to please
hold your applause
until the end of each round.

1148
01:24:05,974 --> 01:24:08,144
May the best man win.

1149
01:24:13,282 --> 01:24:15,182
Mr. Masoudi, are you ready?

1150
01:24:15,284 --> 01:24:17,194
Mr. Hundert,
I am Julius Caesar.

1151
01:24:17,286 --> 01:24:20,316
[ Laughing ]

1152
01:24:21,824 --> 01:24:24,394
Mr. Caesar,

1153
01:24:24,493 --> 01:24:26,403
of the first eight emperors,

1154
01:24:26,495 --> 01:24:30,065
which name is omitted
from the following list?

1155
01:24:30,166 --> 01:24:32,066
Augustus, Tiberius,

1156
01:24:32,168 --> 01:24:35,838
Caligula, Nero, Vespasian,

1157
01:24:35,938 --> 01:24:39,208
Titus, Domitian.

1158
01:24:41,277 --> 01:24:43,307
Trajan.

1159
01:24:43,412 --> 01:24:46,352
I'm sorry.
That is incorrect.

1160
01:24:46,449 --> 01:24:48,779
Oh.
Was it Marcus Aurelius?

1161
01:24:48,884 --> 01:24:50,794
- No.
- Commodus?

1162
01:24:50,886 --> 01:24:52,916
- No.
- Oh. Septimus?

1163
01:24:53,021 --> 01:24:55,791
Nope.
Gaseous Flatulus?

1164
01:24:58,527 --> 01:25:00,357
I'm sorry.
None of the above.

1165
01:25:00,463 --> 01:25:03,433
[ Clears Throat ] The question
goes to Mr. Mehta. Would you
like me to repeat the question?

1166
01:25:03,532 --> 01:25:05,902
No, sir.
It's Claudius, sir.
That is correct.

1167
01:25:06,001 --> 01:25:07,941
- See, I knew that.
- It was a short reign,
but a happy one.

1168
01:25:08,036 --> 01:25:10,136
You may be seated, Mr. Masoudi.

1169
01:25:12,608 --> 01:25:15,608
Thank you.
Thank you for your kindness.

1170
01:25:15,711 --> 01:25:18,511
[ Man ]
That's all right, Lou.

1171
01:25:18,614 --> 01:25:20,524
Now you can
check your messages.

1172
01:25:20,616 --> 01:25:22,546
Uh, Mr. Bell,

1173
01:25:22,651 --> 01:25:27,261
please translate,
if you will,
alea jacta est.

1174
01:25:27,356 --> 01:25:30,026
Tell me who uttered these words,
and on what occasion.

1175
01:25:30,125 --> 01:25:34,425
Alea jacta est.
"The die is cast."

1176
01:25:34,530 --> 01:25:37,870
Caesar spoke these words
as he crossed the Rubicon
in defiance of his government.

1177
01:25:37,966 --> 01:25:40,636
That is correct.

1178
01:25:43,239 --> 01:25:45,569
I've been studying.
I can see that.

1179
01:25:47,042 --> 01:25:49,082
Mr. Mehta,

1180
01:25:49,178 --> 01:25:52,748
what year was the Roman army
crushed at Lake Trasimene?

1181
01:25:52,848 --> 01:25:54,748
217 B.C., sir.

1182
01:25:54,850 --> 01:25:57,820
Correct.

1183
01:25:57,920 --> 01:26:00,460
Mr. Bell, who was
the last emperor...

1184
01:26:00,556 --> 01:26:02,456
of the Western Empire?

1185
01:26:12,801 --> 01:26:14,841
Romulus Augu--

1186
01:26:16,405 --> 01:26:20,235
[ Clears Throat ]
Romulus Augustulus.

1187
01:26:20,343 --> 01:26:22,413
Correct.

1188
01:26:29,885 --> 01:26:31,785
- Mr. Mehta.
- Milvian Bridge.

1189
01:26:31,887 --> 01:26:33,787
- Correct.
- Lex Vatinia.

1190
01:26:33,889 --> 01:26:35,759
- Very good.
- Praetorian Guard.

1191
01:26:35,858 --> 01:26:38,358
- [ Bell ]
Marcus Aurelius.
- Theodosius II.

1192
01:26:38,461 --> 01:26:40,461
- That is correct.
- Comitia Tributa.

1193
01:26:40,563 --> 01:26:42,603
- The Hadrianic rotunda.
- Well done.

1194
01:26:49,405 --> 01:26:51,305
Mr. Bell,

1195
01:26:51,407 --> 01:26:55,377
which tribes
invaded Rome in 102 B.C.?

1196
01:26:58,013 --> 01:26:59,983
Um--

1197
01:27:00,082 --> 01:27:01,952
[ Hundert ]
Take your time.

1198
01:27:04,119 --> 01:27:06,019
The Teutons.

1199
01:27:06,121 --> 01:27:08,721
Oh, um--
God.

1200
01:27:11,026 --> 01:27:13,826
Um--

1201
01:27:16,899 --> 01:27:18,729
[ Sighs ]

1202
01:27:37,686 --> 01:27:39,886
I'm gonna take a stab here.
Uh--

1203
01:27:45,361 --> 01:27:48,301
The Cimbri?

1204
01:27:48,397 --> 01:27:51,297
That is correct.

1205
01:27:58,006 --> 01:28:00,676
Well done.
I'm impressed.

1206
01:28:02,711 --> 01:28:06,281
Thank you very much.
Good. I'm--

1207
01:28:22,731 --> 01:28:25,931
Mr. Mehta--
[ Clears Throat ]

1208
01:28:26,034 --> 01:28:28,774
What was Caracalla's
greatest constit--

1209
01:28:30,172 --> 01:28:33,442
greatest constitutional reform?

1210
01:28:33,542 --> 01:28:37,952
He granted citizenship
to all free male inhabitants
of the empire.

1211
01:28:38,046 --> 01:28:40,476
That is correct.

1212
01:28:46,855 --> 01:28:48,685
Mr. Bell,

1213
01:28:53,762 --> 01:28:55,662
who was Shutruk-Nahhunte?

1214
01:28:55,764 --> 01:28:58,574
[ Chuckling ]

1215
01:29:11,614 --> 01:29:15,354
Shutruk-Nahhunte, huh?

1216
01:29:21,123 --> 01:29:23,533
Come on, Bell.
It was above the damn door.

1217
01:29:23,626 --> 01:29:27,256
The door!
He's gotta know this.

1218
01:29:27,362 --> 01:29:29,432
The door, Sedgewick!
Come on!

1219
01:29:32,801 --> 01:29:34,471
Come on, Sedgewick.

1220
01:29:36,939 --> 01:29:38,769
The plaque!

1221
01:29:44,547 --> 01:29:47,177
I guess if Deepak
can nab this one,

1222
01:29:47,282 --> 01:29:50,122
then it's his ball game.

1223
01:29:57,860 --> 01:30:00,730
Mr. Mehta,
who was Shutruk-Nahhunte?

1224
01:30:00,829 --> 01:30:04,269
Shutruk-Nahhunte,
King of Ansham and Susa,

1225
01:30:04,366 --> 01:30:07,466
sovereign of the land of Elam.

1226
01:30:07,570 --> 01:30:11,110
That is correct.
[ Cheering, Clapping ]

1227
01:30:26,421 --> 01:30:29,531
Ladies and gentlemen,
Mr. Julius Caesar!

1228
01:30:29,625 --> 01:30:31,685
- Hail, Caesar!
- Hail, Caesar!

1229
01:30:33,829 --> 01:30:38,199
- Congratulations. Good job.
-[ Man ]
Hail, Caesar!

1230
01:30:38,300 --> 01:30:40,270
Congratulations, Deepak.

1231
01:30:40,368 --> 01:30:45,008
Thank you very much,
Mr. Hundert.
Good job.

1232
01:30:45,107 --> 01:30:47,837
I suppose if you're gonna lose,
you may as well
lose to the brightest.

1233
01:30:47,943 --> 01:30:50,153
Deepak Mehta.

1234
01:30:53,015 --> 01:30:56,045
And thanks again,
Mr. Hundert.

1235
01:30:56,151 --> 01:30:59,621
Your virtue is
a beacon of light.

1236
01:30:59,722 --> 01:31:03,162
All right, Mr. H.!

1237
01:31:03,258 --> 01:31:06,058
Oh, no, Mr. Hundert.
Please stay.

1238
01:31:07,362 --> 01:31:09,702
And thank you all.

1239
01:31:09,798 --> 01:31:11,698
We've had a great deal
of fun this weekend,

1240
01:31:11,800 --> 01:31:15,240
and we, uh, probably shouldn't
wait another 25 years
till we do it again.

1241
01:31:15,337 --> 01:31:18,037
[ Man ]
I'll say.

1242
01:31:18,140 --> 01:31:22,210
But there's a more serious side
to this gathering.

1243
01:31:22,310 --> 01:31:24,210
One that concerns
every one of us.

1244
01:31:24,312 --> 01:31:26,782
Because all of us here,
I believe,

1245
01:31:26,882 --> 01:31:29,322
care about the quality
of education in this country.

1246
01:31:29,417 --> 01:31:32,447
And not just at places
like Saint Benedict's,

1247
01:31:32,555 --> 01:31:35,815
not just for our children,

1248
01:31:35,924 --> 01:31:40,264
but for the nation's children.

1249
01:31:40,362 --> 01:31:43,832
Because we all care about
our country's future.

1250
01:31:43,932 --> 01:31:45,832
Marty.

1251
01:31:45,934 --> 01:31:48,144
About its moral leadership.

1252
01:31:48,236 --> 01:31:51,366
About its fiscal leadership.

1253
01:31:51,473 --> 01:31:55,483
And because we know that,
as a generation,

1254
01:31:55,578 --> 01:31:57,508
it is our time to lead.

1255
01:32:02,050 --> 01:32:04,520
And why I stand before you now,

1256
01:32:04,620 --> 01:32:08,760
to tell you my intentions
of following in the formidable
footsteps of my father,

1257
01:32:08,857 --> 01:32:12,527
and announce my bid for a seat
in the United States Senate.

1258
01:32:12,628 --> 01:32:16,228
And I want to ask you all
for your support.

1259
01:32:18,601 --> 01:32:20,941
[ Man ]
He's got my vote!

1260
01:32:21,036 --> 01:32:24,336
Congratulations.
Sedgewick, congratulations.

1261
01:32:24,439 --> 01:32:27,079
[ Chattering, Indistinct ]

1262
01:32:50,633 --> 01:32:52,603
Here you are.

1263
01:32:52,701 --> 01:32:55,871
There's about, uh, 30 balding,
middle-aged men out there
looking for you, Professor.

1264
01:32:57,840 --> 01:33:00,580
Well, some things
can't wait.

1265
01:33:02,210 --> 01:33:04,180
[ Sighs ]

1266
01:33:04,279 --> 01:33:06,279
God, you were great
this evening.

1267
01:33:06,381 --> 01:33:09,951
You're like fine wine.
You improve with age.

1268
01:33:10,052 --> 01:33:14,062
[ Urinal Flushes ]

1269
01:33:14,156 --> 01:33:16,456
Everything all right?

1270
01:33:16,558 --> 01:33:18,358
Yes, I'm fine.
Yeah? You don't--

1271
01:33:18,460 --> 01:33:22,630
You don't look so fine.
I'm all right.
Thank you.

1272
01:33:24,499 --> 01:33:26,899
Well, then hurry up
and come join the celebration.

1273
01:33:29,905 --> 01:33:33,005
How long have you been
hard of hearing, Sedgewick?

1274
01:33:36,679 --> 01:33:38,849
[ Sighs ]

1275
01:33:38,947 --> 01:33:42,217
Uh--
[ Chuckles ]

1276
01:33:42,317 --> 01:33:45,217
Very good, Mr. Hundert.
Very--

1277
01:33:45,320 --> 01:33:50,060
Very good. Yeah.

1278
01:33:50,158 --> 01:33:52,858
Boy, I thought
you might've known.

1279
01:33:55,097 --> 01:33:57,697
Who's the poor mercenary
who was feeding you the answers?

1280
01:33:57,800 --> 01:34:02,440
Oh, just some graduate student.
Gave him a couple hundred bucks
and a warm meal.

1281
01:34:02,537 --> 01:34:05,737
I trust you'll keep this
between us.

1282
01:34:05,841 --> 01:34:07,781
As always.

1283
01:34:09,277 --> 01:34:11,747
I trust you will.

1284
01:34:14,316 --> 01:34:18,716
Do you mean am I going
to go out there...

1285
01:34:18,821 --> 01:34:21,461
and expose you
for a liar and a cheat?

1286
01:34:21,556 --> 01:34:23,386
No.

1287
01:34:24,827 --> 01:34:26,657
I'm a teacher, Sedgewick.

1288
01:34:26,762 --> 01:34:30,402
And I failed you--

1289
01:34:30,498 --> 01:34:33,468
As a teacher.

1290
01:34:33,568 --> 01:34:36,098
But I will give you
one last lecture,

1291
01:34:36,204 --> 01:34:38,214
if I may.

1292
01:34:38,306 --> 01:34:40,306
All of us,

1293
01:34:40,408 --> 01:34:44,008
at some point,
are forced...

1294
01:34:44,112 --> 01:34:46,552
to look at ourselves
in the mirror,

1295
01:34:46,648 --> 01:34:49,118
and see who we really are.

1296
01:34:49,217 --> 01:34:51,917
And when that day
comes for you, Sedgewick,

1297
01:34:52,020 --> 01:34:54,990
you will be confronted
with a life...

1298
01:34:55,090 --> 01:34:57,190
lived without virtue,

1299
01:34:57,292 --> 01:35:01,462
without principle,

1300
01:35:01,563 --> 01:35:04,033
and for that I pity you.

1301
01:35:04,132 --> 01:35:05,972
End of lesson.

1302
01:35:09,537 --> 01:35:12,807
Well, can I say,
Mr. Hundert,

1303
01:35:12,908 --> 01:35:14,808
who gives a shit?

1304
01:35:14,910 --> 01:35:16,810
Honestly.

1305
01:35:16,912 --> 01:35:20,452
Who out there
gives a shit...

1306
01:35:20,548 --> 01:35:24,418
about your principles
and your virtues?

1307
01:35:24,519 --> 01:35:27,259
I mean, look at you.

1308
01:35:27,355 --> 01:35:29,185
What do you have
to show for yourself?

1309
01:35:31,827 --> 01:35:36,097
I live in the real world,
where people do what they need
to do to get what they want.

1310
01:35:36,198 --> 01:35:39,128
And if it's lying
and it's cheating,
then so be it.

1311
01:35:39,234 --> 01:35:41,544
So, I am gonna
go out there,

1312
01:35:41,636 --> 01:35:44,266
and I am going to win
that election, Mr. Hundert,

1313
01:35:44,372 --> 01:35:46,312
and you will
see me everywhere.

1314
01:35:48,977 --> 01:35:50,847
And I'll worry
about my contribution later.

1315
01:35:50,946 --> 01:35:52,816
[ Toilet Flushing ]

1316
01:35:59,554 --> 01:36:01,764
Robert.

1317
01:36:05,027 --> 01:36:06,897
Robert.

1318
01:36:44,132 --> 01:36:47,042
And a, uh,
vodka tonic, please.
Yes, sir.

1319
01:36:48,904 --> 01:36:52,044
Oh, Mr. Hundert.
Martin, could I
have a minute?

1320
01:36:52,140 --> 01:36:54,910
Oh, sure, sure.
I-I enjoyed that so much, sir.

1321
01:36:55,010 --> 01:36:58,010
I-I-I'm so sorry my wife
and my kids couldn't
be here to see it.

1322
01:36:58,113 --> 01:37:00,423
You know, I really thought
Sedgewick was gonna
take it from him this time.

1323
01:37:00,515 --> 01:37:03,445
It was amazing.
Martin, that was supposed
to be you up there.

1324
01:37:03,551 --> 01:37:06,051
I'm... sorry?

1325
01:37:06,154 --> 01:37:08,124
All those years ago,

1326
01:37:08,223 --> 01:37:10,263
it was supposed to be you.

1327
01:37:11,960 --> 01:37:15,360
Well, no. I-I wasn't
one of the original
three finalists.

1328
01:37:15,463 --> 01:37:17,633
You were.

1329
01:37:17,732 --> 01:37:19,532
I gave your spot away.

1330
01:37:22,037 --> 01:37:25,707
It may not mean
anything to you, but...

1331
01:37:25,807 --> 01:37:28,077
I wanted you
to know that.

1332
01:37:29,945 --> 01:37:31,845
Okay.

1333
01:37:31,947 --> 01:37:34,177
I'm sorry, Martin.

1334
01:37:35,951 --> 01:37:37,851
No, that's-that's okay.

1335
01:37:41,156 --> 01:37:43,356
I-I don't know
what I'm supposed to say.

1336
01:37:45,060 --> 01:37:48,130
Um, I've got to, um,

1337
01:37:48,230 --> 01:37:50,100
you know,
Deepak is waiting for me.

1338
01:37:53,601 --> 01:37:56,401
Will I see you
at breakfast tomorrow?

1339
01:37:56,504 --> 01:37:58,344
Sure.

1340
01:38:00,275 --> 01:38:02,105
Good.

1341
01:38:34,409 --> 01:38:37,879
[ Man ]
And here's the key
you were looking for.

1342
01:38:37,980 --> 01:38:40,180
[ Bell ]
That's-- That's
very important to us.

1343
01:38:40,282 --> 01:38:43,122
[ Phone Ringing ]
I think it should be important
to people in our country.

1344
01:38:44,852 --> 01:38:46,622
I think that it's--
it's important.

1345
01:38:46,721 --> 01:38:50,831
My father, Hyram Bell,
instilled in me a
sense of principles.

1346
01:38:50,925 --> 01:38:53,195
What I think
this country really needs,

1347
01:38:53,295 --> 01:38:55,695
it's that sense of--
of right and wrong.

1348
01:38:55,797 --> 01:38:59,427
[ Phone Ringing ]
And we're, uh, speaking of,
of how we can make this...

1349
01:38:59,534 --> 01:39:02,304
better with education,
to-to reach out...

1350
01:39:02,404 --> 01:39:04,314
and really get
to the children,

1351
01:39:04,406 --> 01:39:06,906
to teach them the sense...

1352
01:39:07,009 --> 01:39:10,179
of principles
and sense of contribution.

1353
01:39:18,420 --> 01:39:20,320
Mr. Hundert.

1354
01:39:20,422 --> 01:39:22,722
Have I come
to the wrong place
for breakfast?

1355
01:39:22,824 --> 01:39:27,164
Not at all.
But unfortunately
you've missed everyone.

1356
01:39:27,262 --> 01:39:29,762
Missed everyone?
Well, they seemed
to be rather in a hurry.

1357
01:39:29,864 --> 01:39:31,804
Some of them
had planes to catch.

1358
01:39:31,899 --> 01:39:34,499
But they did say
to say good-bye to you.

1359
01:39:34,602 --> 01:39:37,812
I'm sure I could find you
something to eat if you'd like.

1360
01:39:37,905 --> 01:39:40,005
No, no. Thank you.

1361
01:39:40,108 --> 01:39:43,408
W-Was there--
Thank you.

1362
01:39:54,556 --> 01:39:57,226
Hail, Caesar!

1363
01:40:08,836 --> 01:40:11,506
[ Chattering, Indistinct ]

1364
01:40:13,308 --> 01:40:15,608
Get a picture really quick.
There it is.

1365
01:40:15,710 --> 01:40:18,310
[ Shutter Clicks ]
Look at him.
He looks great.

1366
01:40:18,413 --> 01:40:20,923
Oh, the bat!
Gimme the bat, the bat.

1367
01:40:21,015 --> 01:40:23,885
Mr.-- Mr. Hundert, we have
something we'd like
to present to you.

1368
01:40:23,985 --> 01:40:26,115
The only man
who could make Homer a hit,

1369
01:40:26,221 --> 01:40:28,591
our slugger, Mr. Hundert.

1370
01:40:28,690 --> 01:40:30,730
[ Laughter ]
Do you remember?

1371
01:40:30,825 --> 01:40:33,955
I remember.
Hey, be careful of
the window over there, Mr. H.!

1372
01:40:34,062 --> 01:40:39,002
Eloquently put, Mr. Masoudi,
et al.
Uh, thank you.

1373
01:40:39,101 --> 01:40:41,671
Deepak, the, uh,
the, uh, plaque.

1374
01:40:44,172 --> 01:40:47,112
"A great teacher has little
external history to record.

1375
01:40:47,209 --> 01:40:50,079
"His life goes over
into other lives.

1376
01:40:50,178 --> 01:40:54,118
"These men are pillars
in the intimate structure
of our schools.

1377
01:40:54,216 --> 01:40:56,976
"They are more essential
than its stones or beams.

1378
01:40:57,085 --> 01:40:59,545
"And they will continue
to be a kindling force,

1379
01:40:59,654 --> 01:41:02,394
and a revealing power
in our lives."

1380
01:41:02,490 --> 01:41:04,830
Thank you, Mr. Hundert.

1381
01:41:04,926 --> 01:41:07,626
Thank you, Deepak.

1382
01:41:07,729 --> 01:41:11,199
I, uh--

1383
01:41:13,435 --> 01:41:16,235
He's speechless
for the first time
in his life.

1384
01:41:18,773 --> 01:41:21,143
Whatever I may have
taught you gentlemen,

1385
01:41:21,243 --> 01:41:23,483
um, many years ago,

1386
01:41:23,578 --> 01:41:25,478
I want to thank you
for this weekend,

1387
01:41:25,580 --> 01:41:28,680
because you
have taught me something...

1388
01:41:28,783 --> 01:41:31,393
of inestimable value.

1389
01:41:31,486 --> 01:41:34,656
Thank you.

1390
01:41:34,756 --> 01:41:37,556
To Mr. Hundert.
To you, sir!

1391
01:41:37,659 --> 01:41:40,899
To Mr. H.!

1392
01:41:40,995 --> 01:41:43,895
Thank you.
Here.
Oh, I'm sorry.

1393
01:41:43,998 --> 01:41:45,968
To you.

1394
01:41:46,067 --> 01:41:48,137
[ Helicopter Whirring ]

1395
01:41:48,236 --> 01:41:52,336
I had come here in the hope
that I had been wrong
about Sedgewick Bell.

1396
01:41:52,440 --> 01:41:54,940
Or rather,
that I had been right--

1397
01:41:55,042 --> 01:41:58,252
right to believe in him
all those years ago.

1398
01:41:58,346 --> 01:42:02,346
But this is a story
without surprises.

1399
01:42:02,450 --> 01:42:04,320
As a student of history,
I could be shocked...

1400
01:42:04,419 --> 01:42:07,889
neither by his audacity,
nor his success.

1401
01:42:07,989 --> 01:42:09,959
I had failed Sedgewick.

1402
01:42:10,057 --> 01:42:11,987
But the worth of a life
is not determined...

1403
01:42:12,093 --> 01:42:15,763
by a single failure,
or a solitary success.

1404
01:42:15,863 --> 01:42:19,573
My other students
taught me that.

1405
01:42:20,802 --> 01:42:22,742
However much we stumble,

1406
01:42:22,837 --> 01:42:24,967
it is a teacher's burden
always to hope...

1407
01:42:25,072 --> 01:42:29,012
that with learning, a boy's
character might be changed.

1408
01:42:29,110 --> 01:42:31,810
And, so,
the destiny of a man.

1409
01:42:37,452 --> 01:42:40,152
[ Boy ]
Good morning, Mr. Hundert.
Good morning.

1410
01:42:45,827 --> 01:42:48,457
[ Hundert ]
Your name, sir?
William Simon, sir.

1411
01:42:48,563 --> 01:42:51,333
- And your name?
- Howard Hollander.

1412
01:42:51,433 --> 01:42:55,043
- And yours, sir?
- George Duncan.

1413
01:42:55,136 --> 01:42:57,166
And your name, sir?
Alec Matthews.

1414
01:42:57,272 --> 01:42:59,742
- And yours, miss?
- Kathryn Scott.

1415
01:42:59,841 --> 01:43:02,941
- And your name?
- Tawana Carver.

1416
01:43:03,044 --> 01:43:06,254
- And yours, sir?
- Steven Wong.

1417
